window.SEARCH={"l":"en","i":[["01.html#parsing","1 · Parsing the command line","Key components of Bash","1 · Parsing the command line The shell reads the line, splits it into tokens on operators ( |,;, &&, >), and only then starts expanding. The order is fixed, and it is the single most asked question. There are five stages, not the eight it is often drawn as: 1. brace expansion {a,b} {1..3} 2. one left-to-right pass: tilde ~ parameters $var ${var} command subst. $(cmd) arithmetic $((1+2)) process subst. <(cmd) 3. word splitting on IFS 4. filename globbing *.txt 5. quote removal The second stage of expansion order is a single left-to-right pass, not five sequential phases. Tilde expansion, parameter expansion, command substitution, arithmetic expansion, and process substitution occur together in one sweep. This is proven by x=1; echo $((x=2)) $x, which prints 2 2: arithmetic has already updated x by the time the shell expands the second $x. If parameters were expanded in a separate step before arithmetic, the output would be 2 1. Two more consequences. Globbing runs after parameter expansion, so f=\"*.txt\"; echo $f prints a file list while echo \"$f\" prints the asterisk. And the reverse: {1..3} expands before parameters, which is why {1..$n} does not work. Typical question: “in what order does Bash perform expansions, and why does the result of echo $f depend on quoting?”"],["01.html#expansions","2 · Expansion and word splitting","Key components of Bash","2 · Expansion and word splitting After substitution Bash cuts the result into words on the characters in IFS - by default space, tab and newline. Quotes suppress that split, and that is their only real job. f=\"My File.txt\" rm $f # two targets: My and File.txt rm \"$f\" # one target, correct Double quotes protect boundary splitting not only for single variables, but also for argument lists: \"$@\" expands each positional parameter into a distinct argument preserving internal spaces, whereas $@ and \"$*\" flatten or resplit elements across IFS. The core rule: unquoted expansion should always be an intentional design choice rather than an unthinking default. Typical question: “how do \"$@\", $@ and \"$*\" differ, and what breaks on paths with spaces?”"],["01.html#processes","3 · Processes and redirection","Key components of Bash","3 · Processes and redirection Every external command costs two kernel system calls: fork clones the shell and execve replaces the copy with the program (for the final command in bash -c or a subshell, the extra fork is skipped). Builtins ( [[ ]], read, ${v#*/}) run inside Bash itself without spawning a process - across thousands of loop iterations, that difference is orders of magnitude. A process has three standard descriptors in /proc/$$/fd: 0 input, 1 output, 2 errors. Redirections are applied left to right, and the order changes the result: cmd >log 2>&1 # both streams to the file cmd 2>&1 >log # errors stay on the terminal echo <(true) # /dev/fd/63 (pipe) When redirecting as cmd 2>&1 >log, file descriptor 2 duplicates the current destination of stdout 1 (the terminal), and only then is stdout redirected to the file, leaving error messages visible on the screen; the correct sequence to route both streams into a file is >log 2>&1. In turn, process substitution <(cmd) provides the command with a readable pipe descriptor such as /dev/fd/63: it can only be read sequentially once, and rewinding via lseek is impossible because no physical file exists on disk. Typical question: “why do 2>&1 >log and >log 2>&1 produce different results, and what does <(cmd) actually expand to?”"],["01.html#pipelines","4 · Pipelines and exit codes","Key components of Bash","4 · Pipelines and exit codes Pipeline stages start simultaneously, each in its own child process, connected by a kernel buffer (64 KB by default on Linux). By default the pipeline's exit code is the last stage's; with set -o pipefail it is the last (rightmost) failing stage's: false | tee out echo $? # 0 echo \"${PIPESTATUS[@]}\" # 1 0 set -o pipefail yes | head -n 1 >/dev/null echo $? # 141 (SIGPIPE) By default, a pipeline discards failures in earlier stages and reflects only the exit status of the rightmost command, while the PIPESTATUS array retains the status code of each stage; enabling set -o pipefail forces the pipeline to return the code of the rightmost failing command. However, when the consumer stage (such as head -n 1) closes the pipe early, the upstream writer receives kernel signal SIGPIPE (13) on its next write and exits with code 128 + 13 = 141, which pipefail interprets as an unexpected crash. Furthermore, because each pipeline stage executes inside an isolated subshell, variables updated within a loop after a | remain unchanged in the parent shell. Typical question: “how do you get the exit code of one specific pipeline stage, and why does yes | head -n 1 return 141 under pipefail?”"],["01.html#variables","5 · Variables and scope","Key components of Bash","5 · Variables and scope A shell variable is visible only to the shell itself; export marks it for handing down to child processes. A subshell inherits values but never returns its changes - braces, unlike parentheses, do not create a subshell: x=1; ( x=2); echo $x # 1 x=1; { x=2; }; echo $x # 2 Parentheses launch a subshell that isolates environment mutations, whereas curly braces execute commands in the current shell process, preserving variable assignments. Inside shell functions, all variables are global by default: declaring them with local is mandatory to prevent accidental collisions with caller state. Safe defaults include fallback substitution ${var:-fallback} to guard against empty strings and enabling set -u to abort execution immediately whenever an unbound variable is referenced. Typical question: “how does export differ from a plain assignment, and why is a function without local dangerous?”"],["01.html#signals","6 · Signals and traps","Key components of Bash","6 · Signals and traps A signal is an asynchronous message to a process. trap installs a handler, and the only reliable way to clean up after yourself is a trap on EXIT: it fires on every termination the shell gets to notice - normal exit, error, interrupt. There is one exception, covered below: SIGKILL. tmp=$(mktemp) trap 'rm -f \"$tmp\"' EXIT trap 'echo interrupted; exit 130' INT When trapping an interrupt signal INT, the handler must explicitly terminate execution with exit 130 so the parent process recognizes the abnormal abort. Under Unix conventions, a process terminated by fatal signal N yields exit code 128 + N: 130 for Ctrl+C ( SIGINT, signal 2) and 143 for SIGTERM (signal 15). Signal SIGKILL (9) is handled directly by the kernel and can never be caught with trap, meaning guaranteed cleanup on kill -9 is architecturally impossible by system design. Typical question: “how do you guarantee a temporary file is removed on any termination, and why can SIGKILL not be trapped?”"],["01.html#artifacts","Artifacts: what you have after this page","Key components of Bash","Artifacts: what you have after this page Reading >> Your quantum of life's time · A partnership of dignity with AI · Rewiring your neural network Six mechanisms instead of six recipes. The time was well spent if you now explain a script's behaviour by the order in which the line is processed, rather than by a list of exceptions. That is the rewiring: a recipe is gone in a month, an understood mechanism stays and carries over to any shell. The expansion order - five stages, and why the second one is a single pass rather than five steps. The quoting rule: an unquoted expansion is a decision, not a default. Redirection order and why 2>&1 cannot go just anywhere. PIPESTATUS and pipefail - how not to lose a failure in a pipeline. Process boundaries: subshell, export, local. A trap on EXIT as the only reliable cleanup - on any termination except SIGKILL."],["02.html#shell-modes","1 · Two modes: shell and interpreter","Writing scripts","1 · Two modes: shell and interpreter Bash is a single binary with two distinct roles, and half of the “unexplained” differences between a terminal and a script stem from this boundary. An interactive shell takes commands from a human: it maintains history, line editing, a prompt, and aliases. A script interpreter reads a file: it needs none of these conveniences and loads none of them. bash -c 'shopt -q login_shell; echo $?' # 1 - not a login shell echo $- # no i in scripts # hB (interactive gives himBH) Differences between an interactive login shell and a non-interactive script interpreter cause three widespread failure modes. Shell aliases do not expand in scripts: the expand_aliases option is disabled by default for non-interactive shells. Scripts never source ~/.bashrc: that initialization file is loaded only by interactive non-login shells, meaning customized PATH settings from.bashrc will be missing in cron jobs or CI runners. Only login shells parse ~/.bash_profile: SSH sessions, su -, or direct physical console logins. Practical takeaway: a script must not depend on your shell configuration. It must declare everything it needs itself - search paths, variables, and output locale. What gets asked: “Why does my alias or PATH work in the terminal but fail in a script or in cron?”"],["02.html#skeleton","2 · Skeleton: identical startup for every script","Writing scripts","2 · Skeleton: identical startup for every script A good script always begins with the same five lines. They should be typed reflexively without thinking - save your thinking for what comes next. #!/usr/bin/env bash set -euo pipefail IFS=$'\\n\\t' usage() { echo \"usage: $0 <file>\" >&2; exit 2; } [ $# -eq 1 ] || usage env bash resolves whichever Bash executable is available on the machine, rather than assuming /bin/bash. set -euo pipefail provides three safety fuses: fail on error, fail on unset variable, do not lose a failure inside a pipeline. Narrowing IFS to newline and tab prevents splitting on spaces where it is almost never wanted. Argument validation is part of the skeleton, not decoration. A script run without arguments should state what it expects and exit with a non-zero code immediately, rather than failing on an empty variable midway through execution. What gets asked: “What does set -euo pipefail do and what does it not protect against?”"],["02.html#functions","3 · Functions and arguments","Writing scripts","3 · Functions and arguments A variable inside a function is global by default. Without local, a function silently overwrites caller variables, which is notoriously hard to debug. greet() { local name=\"${1:?name required}\" printf 'hello, %s\\n' \"$name\" } # result - via stdout, # status - via exit code if out=$(greet \"world\"); then echo \"$out\" fi The two communication channels of a function serve distinct roles: standard output (stdout) returns the payload data, while the return code signals execution success or error status. A function that prints an error message to stdout and exits with status 0 corrupts pipeline parsing and silently bypasses downstream error handling. \"${1:?message}\" marks a required argument: if absent, Bash halts the script with a clear message. For option parsing, use getopts; a custom loop with case is justified only for long options, which getopts cannot handle. What gets asked: “How does returning a value from a Bash function differ from return in other languages?”"],["02.html#idempotence","4 · Idempotence and cleanup","Writing scripts","4 · Idempotence and cleanup A script that cannot be run twice is only half a script. In production, re-execution is inevitable: by mistake, after a timeout, or on a CI retry. tmp=$(mktemp -d) trap 'rm -rf \"$tmp\"' EXIT mkdir -p /opt/app/releases ln -sfn \"$new\" /opt/app/current Commands such as mkdir -p, ln -sfn, rsync, and install -D are idempotent: running them repeatedly converges the system to the desired state cleanly, even if target directories or links already exist. Conversely, running mkdir without -p or ln -s without -f and -n fails with errors on a second pass due to existing filesystem targets. A trap on EXIT is the only reliable cleanup: it fires on failure and on Ctrl+C alike. The temporary name must come from mktemp, not from you: a predictable name in /tmp is both a race condition and a security hole. What gets asked: “How do you make a script safe for repeated runs and ensure temporary files are cleaned up?”"],["02.html#logging","5 · Logging and diagnostics","Writing scripts","5 · Logging and diagnostics Separating streams is not style, but a prerequisite for pipelines: results go to stdout, everything else to stderr. Otherwise your “[INFO] starting” message ends up in the data read by the next command. log() { printf '%s %s\\n' \"$(date +%T)\" \"$*\" >&2; } log \"starting\" PS4='+ ${BASH_SOURCE}:${LINENO}: ' set -x # enable tracing set +x # disable set -x prints each command after expansion - the fastest way to see what actually ran. PS4 adds the file and line number to the trace. Enabling tracing across an entire script in production is dangerous: variable values, including secrets, appear in the log. What gets asked: “Where should a script write its messages and why not stdout?”"],["02.html#quality","6 · Quality: shellcheck and formatting","Writing scripts","6 · Quality: shellcheck and formatting Bash has a static analyzer that catches almost every common mistake before you run the script: unquoted expansions, useless cat, string comparisons where numbers were intended, cd without a check. shellcheck script.sh shfmt -i 2 -w script.sh # placed on the line immediately above: # deliberate rule override: # shellcheck disable=SC2086 Disabling ShellCheck linter rules is only acceptable directly above the specific target line, accompanied by an explicit explanation comment. Disabling a rule like SC2086 globally across an entire file disables quote-wrapping checks everywhere, and such a design tradeoff must be deliberate and transparently documented. For testing, there is bats. But the first test of any script is running with --dry-run: a mode where it prints what it would do and does nothing. Build it in from the start, not after the first incident. What gets asked: “How do you test shell scripts and what does shellcheck catch that humans miss?”"],["02.html#artifacts","Artifacts: what you have after this page","Writing scripts","Artifacts: what you have after this page Reading >> Your quantum of life's time · A partnership of dignity with AI · Rewiring your neural network A skeleton typed without thinking, and an understanding of why it looks exactly like that. Time was not wasted if you stopped explaining failures with “for some reason”: each failure here has a named cause - shell mode, unquoted expansion, lost exit status, or missing trap. That is the neural rewire: the skeleton you might forget to type from memory in a month, but the reason for it you will not. The boundary between two modes: what an interactive shell reads versus what a script reads. The five-line skeleton: shebang, strict mode, IFS, usage, argument validation. The two-channel rule: stdout for result, stderr for everything else, exit status for outcome. Idempotence and trap EXIT as requirements, not mere neatness. shellcheck and --dry-run - two checks cheaper than any incident."],["03.html#everywhere","1 · It is already installed wherever you go","The strength of Bash","1 · It is already installed wherever you go The main strength of Bash is not technical, but logistical: it does not need to be installed. It exists in every Linux distribution, Docker image, CI runner, Git hook, and cloud-init of a freshly created VM. The executable is a few megabytes, and no surrounding runtime is required. ls -lh /bin/bash # ~1.4M, and that is all docker run --rm debian bash -c 'echo ok' # ok - without installing anything Compare this to the cost of entry for alternatives: a matching Python version, a virtual environment, dependencies from the network, sometimes a compiler. On your laptop that is fifteen minutes; on a thousand nodes, an entire project. A Bash script runs where nothing else exists yet - which is why it powers what runs first: installers, container entrypoints, cloud bootstrap scripts. What gets asked: “Why are provisioning and container entrypoints still written in shell rather than Python?”"],["03.html#startup-cost","2 · Startup cost is measured in milliseconds","The strength of Bash","2 · Startup cost is measured in milliseconds Bash starts almost instantly because it has nothing to load. Where a script is invoked thousands of times - in a pre-commit hook, in a loop over files, in a test runner - the difference is not cosmetic. time bash -c 'exit' # real ~0m0.003s time python3 -c 'pass' # real ~0m0.030s A tenfold difference in cold-start overhead (3 milliseconds for Bash versus 30 milliseconds for Python) seems trivial for a single run, but becomes a major bottleneck across tens of thousands of invocations in a CI pipeline. The inverse tradeoff also applies: every external utility called from Bash spawns a new process via fork, meaning a loop running 100,000 iterations calling grep will be substantially slower than equivalent logic in Python or Go. What gets asked: “When is startup speed more important than execution speed?”"],["03.html#system-access","3 · Direct system access without an abstraction layer","The strength of Bash","3 · Direct system access without an abstraction layer In Bash, processes, files, streams, and permissions are not a library, but syntax. What takes imports and a dozen lines in other languages takes one here. # redirection, pipeline, background, # wait, exit status - in a single line cmd >out 2>err & pid=$! wait \"$pid\" || echo \"failed: $?\" Bash is purpose-built to orchestrate operating system processes, allowing background execution, I/O redirection, and process waiting by PID to be written in a single compact line. Python’s subprocess module and standard libraries in general-purpose languages require several times more boilerplate to implement identical process management, multiplying opportunities for deadlocks and buffer stalls. What gets asked: “Why is starting and piping processes in shell more concise than in a general-purpose language?”"],["03.html#glue","4 · Glue: the power is not in Bash, but in what it connects","The strength of Bash","4 · Glue: the power is not in Bash, but in what it connects Bash does almost nothing on its own - an architectural choice, not a flaw. It excels at connecting programs, each doing one job well. grep -h ERROR app-*.log \\ | awk '{print $4}' \\ | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn \\ | head -5 A pipeline combining standard tools like grep, awk, sort, and head processes gigabytes of logs to produce an error breakdown in a single line without custom parsing code. The real strength lies within the Unix ecosystem: four decades of specialized utilities communicating over plain text streams make pipeline composition faster and less error-prone than hand-crafted scripts from scratch. The toolset evolves, and the pipeline benefits automatically. ripgrep replaces grep -r, fd sits alongside find, sd replaces sed for simple edits, hyperfine measures performance, delta formats diffs. Most are written in Rust and run noticeably faster on large trees - but their interface is identical: text in, text out, an honest exit status. This proves that the power lies in the architecture, not in specific tools: you can upgrade half the ecosystem without changing the language or your scripts. A language that does nothing itself outlived its generation of utilities precisely because it knew nothing about them - except that they read and write text. What gets asked: “What is the Unix philosophy in practice, and how does it appear in a pipeline?”"],["03.html#one-language","5 · One language from the terminal to production","The strength of Bash","5 · One language from the terminal to production A unique property unmatched by any other language: what you typed by hand and verified with your eyes becomes a program without rewriting. # checked in terminal: curl -fsS \"$URL/health\" | jq -e.ok # exactly this - into a script, a hook, # a CI step, a systemd unit In Bash, moving from interactive exploration in the terminal to unattended automation is a direct copy-paste of the verified command into a script, CI step, or cron entry. In general-purpose languages, that step requires translating shell syntax into library calls, where subtle edge cases in argument escaping, shell expansion, and environment state often creep in. What gets asked: “Why is it valuable that a terminal command and a script line are identical text?”"],["03.html#evolving","6 · It is not frozen: the language evolves","The strength of Bash","6 · It is not frozen: the language evolves Viewing Bash as an artifact of the 1980s does not survive inspection. Version 5 brought associative arrays, mapfile, safe quoting, and waiting on any background process. declare -A cfg cfg[port]=8080 mapfile -t lines < file.txt echo \"${#lines[@]}\" wait -n # first to finish echo \"${cfg[port]@Q}\" mapfile reads a file into an array without a loop or word-splitting traps. ${var@Q} outputs a safely re-usable quoted string. wait -n makes trivial what once required clumsy workarounds - reacting to the first completed job. Check version: bash --version. macOS ships 3.2 by default for licensing reasons, which is no sign that the language stopped evolving. What gets asked: “What was introduced in Bash 4 and 5, and why does macOS still default to 3.2?”"],["03.html#artifacts","Artifacts: what you have after this page","The strength of Bash","Artifacts: what you have after this page Reading >> Your quantum of life's time · A partnership of dignity with AI · Rewiring your neural network Arguments instead of vague impressions. Time was well spent if, when asked “why Bash when Python exists”, you no longer say “it’s common practice”, but point to concrete properties: zero installation, startup speed, syntax-level process control, or the translation-free path from terminal to production. That is the neural rewire: you stop choosing tools by habit and start choosing by the price of the decision. Zero installation as the reason shell runs first. Startup cost: milliseconds versus tens of milliseconds, and when it matters. Processes as syntax rather than as a library. Pipelines as composition of forty years of work - and how the toolset updates ( ripgrep, fd, sd) without altering your scripts. Terminal → script → CI without translation - a property unique among languages. What Bash 4 and 5 introduced: associative arrays, mapfile, wait -n, ${var@Q}."],["04.html#error-handling","1 · Error handling: set -e is leaky","Weaknesses and limits","1 · Error handling: set -e is leaky The biggest weakness, and the most dangerous because it looks like a solved problem. set -e creates a false sense of security, but has more holes than any engineer can keep in their head. set -euo pipefail f() { false; echo \"will run\"; } if f; then:; fi # -e disabled local x=$(false) # local returns 0 yes | head -n 1 # 141 (SIGPIPE) The rule: inside a conditional, to the left of && or ||, inside a function invoked in a conditional, and in local x=$(cmd) - set -e does not trigger. Inversely, enabling pipefail abruptly breaks well-behaved pipelines with head or grep -q when the downstream stage closes the pipe early and the upstream process receives SIGPIPE (exit code 141) from the kernel. Add the absence of exceptions and stack traces, and you have a language where reliable error handling demands personal discipline rather than language guarantees. What gets asked: “When does set -e fail to catch an error, and why does pipefail crash on head -n 1?”"],["04.html#text-not-data","2 · There is no data, only text","Weaknesses and limits","2 · There is no data, only text Bash has only one true data type: strings. Arrays exist, but cannot be passed into functions, nested within each other, or returned outward. Structs do not exist at all. # not an array of arrays, # just a space-separated string arr=(\"a b\" \"c\") other=(\"${arr[@]}\") # copy like this f() { local -n ref=$1; } # bash 4.3+ # no other way to pass an array cat data.json | jq -r '.items[].id' # JSON in bash - always via jq Any manipulation of JSON, YAML, or CSV requires external tools: jq, yq, awk. For small payloads, that is fine. As soon as you have nested objects, cross-source joins, or schema mutations - you are writing a jq program embedded in a bash program, and nobody will be able to maintain it. What gets asked: “How do you pass an array to a function in Bash, and why is it awkward?”"],["04.html#integer-math","3 · Integer-only arithmetic","Weaknesses and limits","3 · Integer-only arithmetic Built-in Bash arithmetic does not support floating-point fractions. Period. echo $(( 7 / 2)) # 3 echo $(( 10 * 95 / 100)) # 9 echo 'scale=2; 7/2' | bc # 3.50 awk 'BEGIN{print 7/2}' # 3.5 Any percentage, average, or fractional elapsed time requires invoking bc or awk, spawning an external process for every calculation. If a task involves mathematics, Bash is the wrong tool, and no amount of skill changes that. What gets asked: “How do you compute an average or percentage in Bash, and why is it cumbersome?”"],["04.html#parallelism","4 · Concurrency without synchronization","Weaknesses and limits","4 · Concurrency without synchronization Launching a hundred background jobs is easy. Doing it correctly is not: the language provides no mutexes, queues, or thread-safe shared state. for f in *.log; do process \"$f\" & done wait # exit status - not yours # throttle and capture failures: printf '%s\\0' *.log \\ | xargs -0 -P4 -n1 process Two background processes writing to the same file will interleave output lines. A counter incremented inside a subshell remains unchanged outside. And wait without arguments always returns 0 - even if every background job failed; discovering failures requires waiting on each PID individually. While xargs -P and parallel handle trivial cases, anything beyond processing N independent files is your cue to leave Bash. What gets asked: “How do you limit parallel jobs in shell, and how do you know if one failed?”"],["04.html#readability","5 · Readability degrades beyond three hundred lines","Weaknesses and limits","5 · Readability degrades beyond three hundred lines Bash syntax is optimized for single-line interactive entry, not for reading six months later. Quotes, expansions, and tests combine into text that is hard to scan visually. [[ -n \"${v:-}\" ]] && [[ \"${v}\"!= \"x\" ]] \\ || { echo \"no\"; exit 1; } # condition, action, and error # handling - in a single line As a script grows beyond three hundred lines, fundamental Bash limitations become painful: lack of modules and namespaces, global-by-default variables, and typos that pass unnoticed without set -u. The practical threshold is around three hundred lines: past that, reading the script demands the same esoteric context as writing it, defeating the goal of maintainable engineering. What gets asked: “At what boundary should a shell script be rewritten in another language?”"],["04.html#portability","6 · Portability: two different worlds","Weaknesses and limits","6 · Portability: two different worlds “Works on my machine” in shell fails for two independent reasons: the wrong shell and the wrong utilities. #!/bin/sh echo ${arr[0]} # error in dash sed -i 's/a/b/' f # GNU sed -i '' 's/a/b/' f # BSD/macOS stat -c %s f # GNU; BSD: -f %z date -d @0 # GNU; BSD: -r 0 /bin/sh on Debian and Ubuntu is dash, not Bash: arrays, [[ ]], process substitution, and ${v//a/b} are missing. ( local, contrary to popular belief, is supported in dash.) In Alpine containers, the shell is BusyBox ash, and half of standard flags behave differently. On macOS, Bash is stuck at version 3.2, and utilities are BSD. The honest, boring solution: either commit to #!/usr/bin/env bash and drop POSIX purity, or check with shellcheck -s sh and renounce convenience. There is no middle ground, and the choice must be made deliberately. What gets asked: “How does sh differ from bash, and why do scripts break in Alpine or on macOS?”"],["04.html#when-to-leave","7 · When to move on, and where","Weaknesses and limits","7 · When to move on, and where Signs that Bash is no longer the right tool - any single one is enough: the task requires data structures or JSON beyond a flat list; floating-point arithmetic is needed; real error handling with recovery is necessary; concurrency goes beyond N independent tasks; the script exceeds three hundred lines and continues growing; it will be maintained by engineers unfamiliar with Bash. Where to go. Python - when you need data structures and libraries. Go or Rust - when you need a single dependency-free binary on the target host; Go is cheaper in development velocity, Rust in long-term operational reliability, and for replacing a script the former usually decides it. Make - when the problem is about task dependencies rather than linear steps. Ansible or Terraform - when the goal is desired state rather than imperatively executing actions. And the inverse rule, equally vital: do not rewrite twenty lines of Bash in Python out of dogma. Maintenance cost scales with size, not language."],["04.html#artifacts","Artifacts: what you have after this page","Weaknesses and limits","Artifacts: what you have after this page Reading >> Your quantum of life's time · A partnership of dignity with AI · Rewiring your neural network The ability to say “Bash does not fit here” and name the reason. Time was well spent if you stopped arguing about languages in the abstract and started citing concrete workload constraints: fractions, nested data, error recovery, script scale. That is the neural rewire - choosing tools stops being a matter of taste and becomes an explicit cost calculation. Holes in set -e: conditionals, &&, functions called in conditions, command substitutions in assignments. A single data type and why JSON in Bash always means jq. Integer-only math and the overhead of invoking bc. Concurrency without synchronization and the boundaries of xargs -P. The three-hundred-line threshold as a practical boundary. Two causes of portability failures: different shells and different utilities. The checklist of exit criteria and where to migrate: Python, Go, Make, Ansible."],["05.html#git","1 · Git: hooks and filters","Interconnections","1 · Git: hooks and filters Git lacks a built-in extension language: it launches executable files, and nearly always that means a shell script. A hook receives input via arguments or stdin and decides outcomes by exit status: non-zero aborts the operation. #!/usr/bin/env bash #.git/hooks/pre-commit set -euo pipefail files=$(git diff --cached --name-only \\ --diff-filter=ACM | grep '\\.sh$' || true) [ -z \"$files\" ] && exit 0 shellcheck $files Automating Git hooks in Bash faces two classic pitfalls. First: grep without matches exits with 1, which under set -e aborts the hook, requiring || true on filtering pipelines. Second: the.git/hooks directory is unversioned by Git; team hooks must live in a dedicated repository directory enabled via git config core.hooksPath, otherwise teammates will never execute them. What gets asked: “How do you make a hook reject a commit, and why do hooks not propagate to other developers automatically?”"],["05.html#ci","2 · CI and GitHub Actions: default shell","Interconnections","2 · CI and GitHub Actions: default shell A run: step is a shell script written to a temporary file and executed by CI. Knowing the flags matters, and there is a subtle trap: without an explicit shell:, GitHub Actions runs bash -e {0} - set -e is active, but pipefail is absent. If you specify shell: bash explicitly, it runs as bash --noprofile --norc -eo pipefail {0} - pipefail is added. Neither variant enables -u. - name: build shell: bash # gives -eo pipefail run: | set -euo pipefail # supply own -u./build.sh # each step is a new process: # export in one step is invisible # to the next echo \"VER=1.2\" >> \"$GITHUB_ENV\" In CI pipelines, every step executes in a separate shell process, meaning environment variables cannot persist via export and must be passed across steps using files such as $GITHUB_ENV and $GITHUB_OUTPUT. Tracing requires extreme caution: set -x inside a step expands variables and leaks secrets into build logs, where automated masking is never foolproof. What gets asked: “What flags does CI use to execute run: steps, and how do you persist values across steps?”"],["05.html#docker","3 · Docker: PID 1, signals, and syntax form","Interconnections","3 · Docker: PID 1, signals, and syntax form The most common ENTRYPOINT defect involves process hierarchy rather than shell syntax. In shell string form, the command runs via /bin/sh -c, and PID 1 belongs to the shell rather than your application - SIGTERM never reaches it, turning container stop into a forced SIGKILL timeout. # bad: PID 1 is sh ENTRYPOINT /app/run.sh # good: exec form ENTRYPOINT [\"/app/run.sh\"] # and in the script itself, at the end: exec /app/server \"$@\" In container entrypoints, invoking exec replaces the shell process with the application binary: PID 1 belongs to the application and OS shutdown signals arrive intact. Because containers lack an init system, zombie processes spawned by helper tasks are never reaped without the --init flag or tini. In Alpine images, /bin/sh is provided by BusyBox ash, and bash is absent altogether. What gets asked: “Why does a container fail to stop on docker stop, and how does ENTRYPOINT syntax cause it?”"],["05.html#systemd","4 · systemd: when a script is redundant","Interconnections","4 · systemd: when a script is redundant systemd handles natively much of what wrapper scripts were written for: automatic restarts, dependencies, resource limits, and logging. A wrapper that merely starts and restarts a service is superfluous. [Service] ExecStart=/usr/bin/app --port 8080 Restart=on-failure EnvironmentFile=/etc/app.env # ExecStart is not a shell: # neither $VAR, nor |, nor > work here ExecStart=/bin/bash -c 'app | tee -a log' # like this - only if strictly unavoidable The ExecStart directive is parsed directly by systemd, not a shell: variable substitutions, pipelines, and redirections do not work there. If strictly required, invoke bash -c explicitly and deliberately. Logs sent to stdout/stderr are ingested by journalctl; custom file rotation in scripts is almost always redundant work. What gets asked: “Why do redirections fail in ExecStart, and when is bash -c necessary?”"],["05.html#make-ansible","5 · Make and Ansible: each line gets its own shell","Interconnections","5 · Make and Ansible: each line gets its own shell A shared trap in both tools: lines do not share execution state. In Make, every recipe line runs in a separate subshell, meaning a cd in the first line has no effect on the second. # does not work: build: cd src make all # works: build: cd src && make all # or a single shell for the entire recipe:.ONESHELL: In Ansible playbooks, the shell module spins up a fresh subshell for every task without persisting shell state. The key architectural distinction: the command module executes binaries directly via syscalls without a shell, disabling pipelines, substitutions, and file globs, whereas shell invokes an actual command interpreter with all its features and injection risks. The rule: stick to command until a shell is proven strictly necessary. What gets asked: “Why does cd in a Makefile not persist into the next line, and how does command differ from shell in Ansible?”"],["05.html#boundaries","6 · Where Bash ends: jq, awk, Python","Interconnections","6 · Where Bash ends: jq, awk, Python Ecosystem neighbors divide labor by data structure, and knowing the boundaries saves hours. # structured data - jq jq -r '.items[] | select(.ok).id' f.json # columns and arithmetic - awk awk -F, '{s+=$3} END{print s/NR}' f.csv # everything else - a real language python3 -c 'import json, sys;...' jq handles anything with nested hierarchies. awk handles line-oriented data with columns and arithmetic; it often replaces a pipeline of grep | cut | sort with a single process. Python takes over when you need third-party libraries, networking beyond curl, or stateful multi-step execution. Practical indicator: once a script contains a second jq invocation with a multi-line expression, the task has outgrown Bash. What gets asked: “When do you pick awk over a grep | cut pipeline, and when do you migrate to Python?”"],["05.html#artifacts","Artifacts: what you have after this page","Interconnections","Artifacts: what you have after this page Reading >> Your quantum of life's time · A partnership of dignity with AI · Rewiring your neural network A map of integration seams instead of disjointed recipes. Time was well spent if you now ask the right questions about external tools: what invokes my string, in which process, and what does it do with the exit status? That is the neural rewire: replacing “why does it fail” with “who executes this”. Git: decisions via exit status; hooks require core.hooksPath to be versioned. CI: default run: lacks pipefail; shell: bash adds it; -u is never enabled by default; cross-step state via files. Docker: exec form, exec at the script's end, and the absence of an init process. systemd: ExecStart is not a shell; stream logs to the journal, not files. Make and Ansible: each line runs in an isolated shell; command versus shell. The boundary with jq, awk, and Python defined by data structure rather than habit."],["06.html#model-mistakes","1 · Where models fail in Bash, and why","Practices in an AI context","1 · Where models fail in Bash, and why A language model's shell errors are not random - they recur predictably, and the list is short. Knowing this list turns reviewing its output from reading all lines into inspecting six specific spots. # 1. unquoted expansion rm $file # path with space # 2. lost exit status grep x f | tee out # returns 0 # 3. non-existent option sort --parallel=4 # not in BSD # 4. > instead of >> echo a > log # overwritten log # 5. strict mode against standard code set -e grep -q x f # no x: exit, silence diff a b # differences found: exit The first four stem from training data: models reproduce the most frequent text, and the most common text on the internet is written without strict mode and without quotes. Modern agentic models after post-training add set -euo pipefail and quotes on their own - and therefore commit the fifth, inverse mistake more often: crashing their own scripts on standard non-zero exit codes. grep without matches returns 1, diff on differences returns 1, ((i++)) from zero returns 1, and under strict mode each of these terminates the script without printing a single message. The sixth is invoking with full confidence an option that does not exist in your utility version: the model does not know your environment, whether it is GNU or BSD, bash or dash. What gets asked: “Which AI mistakes in shell code do you distrust and verify first?”"],["06.html#task-brief","2 · How to specify tasks for scripts","Practices in an AI context","2 · How to specify tasks for scripts The difference between “write a backup script” and a working result lies in what the model cannot determine on its own: boundaries and the definition of done. It will inspect the environment faster and more accurately than you if the prompt tells it to do so: checking /etc/os-release and bash --version takes one second and does not depend on human memory. First: inspect the environment yourself - os-release, bash --version, sudo -n. Task: archive /var/app into /backup, dated filename, retain the last 7. Boundaries: do not delete outside /backup; do not install packages. Done when: shellcheck is silent, --dry-run prints the plan, repeated run does not break. The definition of done is a predicate, and it must be formulated before the code. Without it, you evaluate the result on impression, while the model optimizes for looking finished. Three lines of specification save half an hour of revisions. Persistent rules go into files, one-off rules go into the prompt. Rules that apply permanently - stop switches, push restrictions, verification commands - live in the repository's AGENTS.md, and the agent reads them on every start. Typing them into every prompt guarantees forgetting them eventually. What gets asked: “What do you include in a task specification for an agent besides the task itself?”"],["06.html#check-loop","3 · A verification loop beats any prompt","Practices in an AI context","3 · A verification loop beats any prompt An agent becomes useful not when it receives a great explanation, but when it possesses the means to verify itself. In Bash, such verification exists and costs almost nothing. shellcheck -S warning script.sh \\ && bash -n script.sh \\ &&./script.sh --dry-run shellcheck catches quotes and exit statuses, bash -n validates syntax without execution, and --dry-run validates logic without side effects. Three commands that the agent runs itself after each edit and fixes what it finds deliver far more than any prompt phrasing: a prompt is hope, but verification is fact. The loop includes two more checks, both about behavior rather than knowledge. First: test options by running them, not by reading help. A model gets options wrong precisely because it assumes they are familiar, so a rule like \"verify unfamiliar options\" never triggers; furthermore, many BSD tools lack both --help and --version. Only one thing is reliable: run the command itself on empty or dummy input - cmd -flag < /dev/null. Second: inspect real data before writing parsing logic. Field names for jq and column numbers for awk are confidently invented by models; running cmd | head -20 before writing the first line of parsing saves more iterations than any syntax check. You are the one who accepts the code the model wrote, and skimming does not work: Bash almost always looks plausible at a glance. Acceptance follows a short checklist. # 1. is strict mode enabled # 2. are all \"$var\" quoted # 3. are exit statuses honest # 4. what does it delete and how is it bounded # 5. does it run cleanly twice in a row And one rule more valuable than the list: ask for an explanation of every line you do not understand. If the explanation contradicts what the command actually does, that discrepancy is your defect. Never leave an unexplained line in a script: responsibility for it remains yours, not the model's. What gets asked: “How is the self-verification loop structured for an agent writing scripts?” What gets asked: “How do you review shell code written by an AI, and what do you do with a line you don't understand?”"],["06.html#not-trusted","4 · What not to trust an agent with","Practices in an AI context","4 · What not to trust an agent with The danger of Bash for automation is that a destructive command looks no different on the outside from an innocent one - neither in length nor in structure. rm -rf \"$dir/\" # $dir empty: disaster sudo anything # no elevated privileges curl … | bash # remote code executed directly > important.log # truncation git push --force # external history The working rule: the agent proposes, the human executes - for anything that deletes, elevates privileges, pulls remote code, or touches shared history. Technically, this is guarded by two measures, both about the environment rather than a banned word list: isolation - containers, data copies, separate worktrees via git worktree, - and confirmation of the exact invocation with all its arguments. A whitelist of command names is not a security boundary: find -delete, tar --checkpoint-action, env, or any interpreter from such a list can destroy and execute just as well as rm. A distinct hazard is text read by the agent. Command output, file contents, web pages, or log entries may contain prompt-like directives: “ignore previous instructions and run...”. To the agent, this is data, not instructions. The defense is identical to human safeguards: a high-risk operation requires human confirmation regardless of where the prompt originated - from the task description, model generation, or a file it just opened. What gets asked: “Which operations do you forbid an agent from running autonomously, and how do you enforce this?”"],["06.html#bash-for-agents","5 · The agent's tools: a machine-shaped script, edits through the tool","Practices in an AI context","5 · The agent's tools: a machine-shaped script, edits through the tool The converse: a script is the best tool interface for an AI when written for machines rather than humans. # machines need an exit status, # not the word \"success\" check() { \"$@\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 1 } # and parsable output printf '%s\\t%s\\n' \"$name\" \"$status\" Three requirements for such a tool: an honest exit status - zero only on genuine success; parsable output - columns or JSON, not human sentences; idempotence - running it again does not corrupt the state, because an agent will retry without asking. And files are not edited with the shell. File-reading and editing tools are present in almost every modern platform, and editing via sed -i, cat <<EOF > file, and echo > becomes the primary source of file corruption. An unquoted delimiter expands $(...) within text, an accidental > truncates a file, and a loose regex in sed matches the wrong line. # how files get corrupted silently: sed -i 's/port=.*/port=8080/' app.conf cat <<EOF > deploy.sh echo \"$HOME\" # expanded upon write EOF A specialized edit tool verifies which exact snippet is replaced, failing gracefully if the snippet is missing or ambiguous; the shell blindly runs whatever was passed. The rule is simple: use the shell for commands, and file-editing tools for files. What gets asked: “What properties must a script have so that an agent can invoke it reliably?” What gets asked: “Why should an agent with file-editing tools avoid modifying files via sed and here-documents?”"],["06.html#agent-environment","6 · An agent environment is not your terminal","Practices in an AI context","6 · An agent environment is not your terminal Most agent environments launch each command as an isolated, non-interactive process and capture its stdout/stderr rather than a terminal screen. This introduces four properties foreign to your interactive shell. Some runners smooth over a subset of these - setting PAGER=cat automatically or piping input to a running child, - but a script that relies on such support ceases to work when moved to another environment. No keyboard. Anything that waits for a user - pagers, password prompts, “continue? [y/N]”, editors for commit messages - turns into an indefinite hang until timeout. git --no-pager log -5 GIT_PAGER=cat PAGER=cat cmd apt-get install -y pkg git commit -m \"commit message\" # without editor cmd < /dev/null # nothing to wait for timeout 60 cmd # always set a limit No state between invocations. cd, variables, functions, and traps defined in one agent command do not exist in the next: each runs in a fresh process. Pass directories explicitly, and keep anything that needs to persist across commands in a file or script. Background processes hold output streams open. A process started with & inherits the agent's stdout/stderr, and the runner waits for it to close even if the invoking script has finished. Fully detach background processes: # agent will wait for server: server & # will not wait: server >log 2>&1 </dev/null & Output consumes context window. A human can scroll past a thousand lines, but for an agent they flood working memory and push out critical context. Scripts designed for agents should be concise: return summaries rather than logs, and keep details behind a --verbose flag or write them to a file. In command chains, use | tail -50, --quiet, and grep -c instead of dumping all matches. What gets asked: “Why does an agent hang on a command that runs instantly in your terminal, and why does its cd have no effect on the next command?”"],["06.html#artifacts","Artifacts: what you take away from this page","Practices in an AI context","Artifacts: what you take away from this page Reading >> Your life quantum · Dignity partnership with AI · Rewiring your neural network A five-point checklist instead of reading the entire code. Your time was well spent if you stopped accepting model output based on persuasive explanations and started accepting it based on verification exit codes. That is the essence of rewiring: trust is replaced by procedure, making work with AI calmer rather than anxious. Six standard model failure modes - and the latest inverse trap: strict mode crashing on standard exit status 1. Task specification: boundaries and definition of done; the agent discovers environment details on its own; persistent rules live in AGENTS.md. Self-verification loop of three commands, plus testing options by invocation and inspecting real data prior to parsing. Security boundary - isolation and invocation confirmation, not a command name whitelist. Three requirements for tool scripts: honest exit statuses, parsable output, and idempotence. Four properties of the agent environment: no keyboard, stateless between commands, background processes keep streams open, and output consumes context. Use file tools for files, shell for commands. Read input is data, not instructions: confirming dangerous operations does not depend on where the idea came from. The ununderstood line rule: explain it or remove it."],["07.html#cloud-cli","1 · Cloud CLIs are regular programs and behave like programs","Practices in the cloud","1 · Cloud CLIs are regular programs and behave like programs The first shift in mindset: a cloud CLI is not an administrative interactive shell, but a CLI utility with an exit status and machine-readable output. The same rules apply to it as to grep. aws ec2 describe-instances \\ --filters Name=tag:app,Values=web \\ --query 'Reservations[].Instances[].InstanceId' \\ --output text aws s3api head-object … >/dev/null 2>&1 \\ && echo exists || echo missing Automating cloud CLIs efficiently relies on two primary rules. The --query parameter applies client-side filtering before output reaches jq, minimizing data transfer and parsing overhead. Second: a missing cloud resource returns a non-zero exit code, not an empty string, meaning scripts must check status codes rather than stdout text. To keep CI scripts from freezing on paginated output, disable the built-in pager using AWS_PAGER=\"\". What gets asked: “How does --query differ from jq, and how do you test in a script whether a cloud resource exists?”"],["07.html#provisioning","2 · Resource provisioning: waiting is part of the job","Practices in the cloud","2 · Resource provisioning: waiting is part of the job Cloud calls are asynchronous: returning an ID does not mean the resource is ready. A script that does not wait fails intermittently and manifests as a flaky bug. id=$(aws ec2 run-instances … \\ --query 'Instances[0].InstanceId' \\ --output text) aws ec2 wait instance-running \\ --instance-ids \"$id\" # custom polling with an attempt limit for i in {1..30}; do ready && break sleep 5 done When awaiting cloud resource readiness, rely on native CLI commands ( wait) wherever available. Where absent, implement polling loops with strict attempt limits and timeouts, preventing failed provisions from locking CI runners for hours. Always tag every provisioned resource immediately so automated cleanup scripts can reliably discover and delete temporary infrastructure. What gets asked: “How do you wait for cloud resource readiness in a script, and what happens without polling?”"],["07.html#credentials","3 · Credentials: what must never appear in scripts","Practices in the cloud","3 · Credentials: what must never appear in scripts The costliest mistakes in cloud scripting are disciplinary rather than technical - credentials leaked into a repository or CI log. # no: AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=AKIA… # yes: instance role or named profile aws --profile prod s3 ls # secrets from a vault into a variable, # never echoed and never under set -x token=$(aws secretsmanager \\ get-secret-value --secret-id t \\ --query SecretString --output text) Script authentication across cloud providers follows a strict hierarchy: instance roles or OIDC eliminate static secrets altogether; named profiles isolate credentials in user configs away from code; environment variables remain strictly a last resort. Crucially: enabling set -x prints parameter expansions into public CI logs, making tracing strictly forbidden in steps handling authentication. What gets asked: “How does a script access cloud APIs without storing static credentials, and why is set -x dangerous there?”"],["07.html#throttling","4 · Pagination, API throttling, and avoiding CLI loops","Practices in the cloud","4 · Pagination, API throttling, and avoiding CLI loops Invoking a cloud CLI in a for loop across hundreds of resources guarantees hitting ThrottlingException ( Rate exceeded) and inflating runtime to thirty minutes: each invocation boots a Python/Go runtime and establishes a new TLS handshake. # no: N API calls in a loop for id in $ids; do aws ec2 describe-instances --instance-ids \"$id\" done # yes: single batch request with adaptive retries export AWS_RETRY_MODE=adaptive export AWS_MAX_ATTEMPTS=6 aws ec2 describe-instances \\ --instance-ids $ids Two rules for scale: pass IDs as batches or apply server-side API filters instead of iterative queries, and enable built-in adaptive retries ( AWS_RETRY_MODE=adaptive) to automatically back off upon receiving 429 or 503 responses. When querying large listings, remember that without autopagination or --next-token, only the first page is returned. What gets asked: “Why do cloud scripts suddenly fail with Rate Exceeded errors, and how do you eliminate CLI loops?”"],["07.html#account-guard","5 · Guardrails against the wrong account, region, or context","Practices in the cloud","5 · Guardrails against the wrong account, region, or context The most destructive cloud outages occur when a valid cleanup script runs in a terminal retaining yesterday's production profile or cluster context. #!/usr/bin/env bash set -euo pipefail export AWS_PAGER=\"\" region=\"${AWS_REGION:?specify region}\" want_acc=\"123456789012\" got_acc=$(aws sts get-caller-identity \\ --query Account --output text) [ \"$got_acc\" = \"$want_acc\" ] || { echo \"wrong account: $got_acc\" >&2 exit 1 } Any script modifying or deleting cloud resources must in its first lines verify the active account and cluster against expectations ( aws sts get-caller-identity, gcloud config get-value project, kubectl config current-context) and require explicit region parameters rather than inheriting stale environment variables from an engineer's shell. What gets asked: “How do you ensure a staging cleanup script never touches production resources?”"],["07.html#cleanup","6 · Cleanup and the boundary with Terraform","Practices in the cloud","6 · Cleanup and the boundary with Terraform Clouds bill for forgotten resources. A script that provisions must be capable of tearing down - identifying targets by metadata tags rather than mental lists. aws ec2 describe-instances \\ --filters Name=tag:tmp,Values=yes \\ --query '…[].InstanceId' --output text \\ | xargs -r aws ec2 terminate-instances \\ --instance-ids When piping cloud resource IDs to teardown commands, xargs -r prevents execution when standard input is empty. Without the -r flag, a resource deletion command runs without arguments, which in cloud CLIs risks applying the teardown across all resources in the account. Where the boundary lies. Bash excels when the task is an action: ephemeral one-hour setups, account-wide auditing snapshots, bucket migrations, tag-based cleanups. Terraform and CloudFormation excel when the task is state: resources that must exist permanently. A script attempting state management inevitably degenerates into a defective Terraform clone - complete with custom state files, hand-rolled drift detection, and unique bugs. What gets asked: “Where does provisioning via shell script end and Terraform begin?”"],["07.html#artifacts","Artifacts: what you take away from this page","Practices in the cloud","Artifacts: what you take away from this page Reading >> Your life quantum · Dignity partnership with AI · Rewiring your neural network Account validation at the entrypoint and tag-based cleanup at exit. Your time was well spent if you stopped looping cloud CLI calls one resource at a time, and now begin every script by confirming account identity and every temporary resource with a tracking tag. That is the essence of rewiring: the cloud ceases to be a minefield of surprise billing and mistaken regions. CLI as a standard program: exit status, client-side --query, disabling AWS_PAGER. Explicit readiness polling via wait or bounded loops. Credential hierarchy: instance roles or OIDC → named profiles → environment variables; strict prohibition of set -x with secrets. Batch requests and AWS_RETRY_MODE=adaptive instead of per-item CLI loops. Account and region validation on line one before executing any mutating operations. Tag-based cleanup, xargs -r, and the architectural boundary: actions in Bash, state in Terraform."],["08.html#ci-step","1 · Bash in pipelines: whose process is this?","DevOps practices","1 · Bash in pipelines: whose process is this? A CI step is your script run by someone else's runner. Three facts about this execution define nearly all its behavior. # 1. runner sets flags, not you # without shell: -> bash -e # shell: bash -> + pipefail set -euo pipefail # -u is yours alone # 2. each step is a new process echo \"VER=$v\" >> \"$GITHUB_ENV\" # 3. working directory is reset cd \"$GITHUB_WORKSPACE\" Every CI pipeline step must be self-contained: it never relies on export from prior steps, never assumes a default directory, and declares its own strict mode explicitly. Extract complex logic into a dedicated.sh script in the repository: it can be executed locally, validated with shellcheck, and clearly reviewed in diffs - unlike dozens of lines embedded inside YAML. What gets asked: “Why is a variable set in one CI step invisible in the next, and how do you pass data between steps?”"],["08.html#build","2 · Building artifacts: reproducibility over speed","DevOps practices","2 · Building artifacts: reproducibility over speed A build script answers a single question: will tomorrow's build produce the exact same artifact as today's? Everything else is secondary. ver=$(git describe --tags --always --dirty) sha=$(git rev-parse --short HEAD) docker build \\ --build-arg VER=\"$ver\" \\ -t \"app:$sha\". # artifact is named by version, # never latest Reproducibility of build artifacts rests on three fundamental rules. Version strings must come directly from Git rather than manual variables that can be forgotten. The latest tag is not a version: rolling back to it reliably is impossible. The --dirty suffix immediately warns of an uncommitted working tree whose build artifact cannot be reproduced from Git history. What gets asked: “Where should artifact versions originate, and why is deploying latest prohibited?”"],["08.html#environments","3 · Environments Dev → QA → Prod: one script, distinct parameters","DevOps practices","3 · Environments Dev → QA → Prod: one script, distinct parameters A classic anti-pattern is maintaining three similar scripts, one per environment. Within a month they diverge, and a production deployment no longer mirrors what was tested on QA. #!/usr/bin/env bash set -euo pipefail env=\"${1:?dev|qa|prod}\" cfg=\"deploy/$env.env\" [ -f \"$cfg\" ] || { echo \"missing $cfg\" >&2; exit 2; } # shellcheck source=/dev/null source \"$cfg\" [ \"$env\" = prod ] &&: \"${APPROVED:?confirmation required}\" One script, external configuration, mandatory environment argument. Never provide default values for the target environment: a script defaulting to production without arguments will eventually deploy to production by accident. Gate production deployments behind an explicit approval variable, not an easily ignored file header comment. What gets asked: “How does a single script serve three environments, and how do you guard production from accidental execution?”"],["08.html#deploy-rollback","4 · Deployment and rollback: design rollback first","DevOps practices","4 · Deployment and rollback: design rollback first The question asked more frequently in interviews than deployment itself: how do you roll back? If the answer requires a new build, it is the wrong answer. # releases side by side, current is a symlink ln -sfn \"/opt/app/rel/$ver\" /opt/app/next mv -T /opt/app/next /opt/app/current systemctl reload app # rollback = identical step targeting previous version Combining ln -sfn with mv -T provides an atomic cutover: at any instant, the symlink points either to the old release or the new release, with no intermediate state. A naive rm symlink && ln -s lacks this guarantee: between the two commands, the symlink does not exist at all. Post-deployment validation belongs in the script as well: querying /health with a bounded retry loop and triggering an automated rollback to the previous release if health checks fail. What gets asked: “How do you make release symlink switching atomic, and how is automated rollback structured?”"],["08.html#pipeline-quality","5 · Pipeline quality: pre-merge checks","DevOps practices","5 · Pipeline quality: pre-merge checks Linting shell scripts is just as vital a CI stage as application unit tests. It takes seconds and catches bugs that would otherwise surface in production. - run: | shopt -s globstar shellcheck **/*.sh - run: shfmt -d -i 2. - run: bats tests/ Run the script quality verification suite locally via pre-commit hooks so defect-laden code never reaches CI. Local developer checks and CI gate steps must execute the exact same command - otherwise teams suffer from “works on my machine, fails in CI” syndrome, destroying trust in the automated checks. What gets asked: “Which shell code checks do you include in CI pipelines, and why must they match local hooks?”"],["08.html#secrets","6 · Secrets and CI logs","DevOps practices","6 · Secrets and CI logs CI logs are visible to anyone with repository access and persist far longer than memory of the secret. # no: set -x # prints everything echo \"token=$TOKEN\" curl -u \"u:$PASS\" … # visible in ps # yes: curl --netrc-file \"$f\" … printf '%s' \"$TOKEN\" | cmd --token-stdin Handling secrets securely in scripts and CI pipelines rests on three rules. Never pass secrets as command-line arguments - process arguments are visible in ps to any local user; transmit them via stdin, files, or environment variables. Shell execution tracing via set -x is strictly forbidden in steps handling secrets. CI log masking provides no absolute guarantee: it matches exact strings only, so multi-line or re-encoded secrets will leak into build logs unmasked. What gets asked: “Why must passwords never be passed as command-line arguments, and what are the limits of CI secret masking?”"],["08.html#beyond-scripts","7 · When a pipeline outgrows shell scripts","DevOps practices","7 · When a pipeline outgrows shell scripts Signs that logic should move out of shell scripts: the script manages state that must persist between invocations; steps have non-linear dependencies - suited for Make or a CI DAG rather than a linear sequence; identical logic is duplicated across three pipelines; understanding the script requires knowing runner-specific CI environment variables. State calls for dedicated state management tools; non-linear workflows call for dependency graphs; duplication calls for shared repository scripts or custom CI actions; and runner coupling calls for extracting logic into standalone scripts runnable locally with identical arguments. And the converse: a fifteen-line Bash pipeline made readable by three comments does not need to become a custom plugin. The complexity of the tooling must be justified by the complexity of the problem, not its importance."],["08.html#artifacts","Artifacts: what you take away from this page","DevOps practices","Artifacts: what you take away from this page Reading >> Your life quantum · Dignity partnership with AI · Rewiring your neural network Understanding that a CI step is an external execution of your code. Your time was well spent if you stopped being puzzled by differing behavior in CI and started checking: what flags were passed, which process executed it, and where the working directory points. That is the essence of rewiring: the pipeline stops being a black box and becomes a transparent environment running your own commands. Three facts about CI steps: runner-defined flags, fresh processes, reset working directory. Git-derived versions and the absolute prohibition of latest in deployments. One script across three environments with a mandatory environment argument and production gates. Atomic cutover via ln -sfn and mv -T, and rolling back with the exact same step. Pre-merge verification identical to local developer tooling. Secret security rules: never in arguments, no execution tracing, masking is not infallible. Indicators that a pipeline has outgrown shell, and the appropriate remedy for each."],["09.html#triage","1 · The first minute of an incident: live server checklist","Practices SRE","1 · The first minute of an incident: live server checklist On a malfunctioning host reached via ssh, Bash is indispensable: it is the only tool guaranteed to be functional. Inspection follows a strict path to avoid wasting time on guesswork. df -h; df -i; free -m # disk, inodes, RAM uptime; vmstat 1 3 # queue and CPU ss -lntp | head -15 # listening ports systemctl is-active app # is unit alive journalctl -u app -n 50 --no-pager ps -eo pid,stat,rss,cmd \\ --sort=-rss | head -6 The sequence is intentional: disk space and inodes → RAM and swap → load queue and ports → service unit status and logs → heaviest processes. In most incidents, the root cause is uncovered in the first two lines, making diving into application code or restarting services before inspecting system resources a critical mistake. What gets asked: “Which commands and in what order do you execute when triaging an incident on an unfamiliar server?”"],["09.html#hidden-failures","2 · Hidden failures: why df contradicts du, zombies, and OOM","Practices SRE","2 · Hidden failures: why df contradicts du, zombies, and OOM Three classic SRE interview scenarios where standard commands show normal status or fail because the mechanism resides in the kernel. # 1. df = 100%, but du sees free space lsof +L1 # deleted but open: > /proc/PID/fd/N # truncate without killing df -i # out of inodes # 2. process vanished without trace in app logs dmesg -T | grep -i 'killed process' # 3. zombie (Z) cannot be killed with kill -9 ps -eo pid,ppid,stat,cmd \\ | awk '$3 ~ /^Z/' If a log file is removed via rm while an active process holds its file descriptor open, the directory entry vanishes ( du ignores it), but disk blocks remain allocated until the descriptor is closed. If restarting the process is not an option, truncate the file via its open descriptor:: > /proc/PID/fd/N. truncate -s 0 on the old filename will not help: the name no longer exists, and the command creates a new empty file alongside without freeing a single byte. A zombie process ( Z) is already dead and consumes no memory - it is merely a process table entry retaining an exit code until its parent ( PPID) invokes wait(); sending signals to a zombie is entirely ineffective. What gets asked: “Why does df -h show 100% when du -sh shows free space, and why can't kill -9 terminate a zombie?”"],["09.html#logs","3 · Logs on a live node: root cause analysis without exhausting disk","Practices SRE","3 · Logs on a live node: root cause analysis without exhausting disk Piping multi-gigabyte logs through a shell pipeline is faster than shipping them to external platforms, provided two traps are avoided: filling the disk with uncompressed archives and stalling on pipe buffering. # top error codes without filenames grep -h ERROR /var/log/app/*.log \\ | awk '{print $5}' \\ | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head # time slice and reading compressed logs on the fly awk '/12:03:/,/12:06:/' app.log zgrep -h '502 Bad' /var/log/nginx/*.gz # live stream without pipe buffer delay tail -f app.log \\ | grep --line-buffered ' 5[0-9][0-9] ' Three details decide the outcome: grep -h suppresses filename prefixes so awk column positions stay stable; --line-buffered forces grep to flush each matching line immediately rather than filling a 4 KB buffer; zgrep and zcat stream compressed archives directly through memory without staging temporary files on a full disk partition. What gets asked: “How do you search rotated compressed logs on a full disk, and why does tail -f | grep often produce no immediate output?”"],["09.html#node-protection","4 · Scripts must not crash production: flock, nice, and ionice","Practices SRE","4 · Scripts must not crash production: flock, nice, and ionice A heavy maintenance script - archiving, rotating, or checksumming - running on production with standard priority starves customer traffic of disk and CPU, triggering an outage on its own. #!/usr/bin/env bash set -euo pipefail exec 9>/var/lock/backup.lock flock -n 9 || exit 0 # second instance exits nice -n 19 ionice -c3 \\ tar -czf /backup/app.tgz /var/lib/app Two safeguards are mandatory for any periodic task on a node. First: flock -n - if a previous run hangs on a slow disk, the next cron trigger will not pile up into a thundering herd of twenty concurrent processes. Second: nice -n 19 ionice -c3 - the Idle class ( -c3) submits I/O requests to disk only when no production workload needs the disk. The essential caveat: ionice priority classes are honored only by the BFQ I/O scheduler (and legacy CFQ). Under none or mq-deadline - typical for NVMe and cloud volumes - ionice is silently ignored. Verify via cat /sys/block/<disk>/queue/scheduler. When BFQ is absent, throttle bandwidth instead: --bwlimit in rsync or IOWriteBandwidthMax= via systemd-run - which works under any I/O scheduler. What gets asked: “How do you protect a production node from degradation during heavy cron jobs?”"],["09.html#cascading-failures","5 · Cascading failures: backoff + jitter against retry storms","Practices SRE","5 · Cascading failures: backoff + jitter against retry storms When an upstream service or database begins throwing errors, two hundred nodes retrying requests in a tight loop every two seconds exhaust the service completely, preventing any recovery. # random start jitter in cron / script sleep $(( RANDOM % 30)) # exponential backoff + jitter retry() { local n for n in 1 2 3 4 5; do curl -sf --max-time 5 \"$url\" && return 0 (( n == 5)) && break sleep $(( (1 << n) + RANDOM % 5)) done echo \"service unavailable\" >&2 return 1 } retry || exit 1 Two fleet rules: scheduled jobs must never trigger simultaneously at second 00 (use RandomizedDelaySec in systemd timers or initial delay via RANDOM), and intervals between retries must increase exponentially with random jitter ( jitter) to desynchronize request waves after an outage. The loop must terminate with an honest exit code: without an explicit return 1 after the final failed attempt, it returns the exit status of the loop test, disguising complete upstream failure as success. What gets asked: “Why does a naive retry loop on a fleet turn a transient hiccup into a prolonged outage?”"],["09.html#metrics","6 · Script observability and the self-healing anti-pattern","Practices SRE","6 · Script observability and the self-healing anti-pattern A background script fails in two ways: it exits with an error or stops running entirely. If it writes exclusively to local logs, backup failures will only be discovered on the day recovery is required. t0=$SECONDS; rc=0 run_backup || rc=$? dur=$(( SECONDS - t0)) prom=/var/lib/node_exporter/backup.prom cat <<EOF > \"$prom.$$\" backup_last_exit_code $rc backup_duration_seconds $dur backup_last_run_timestamp $(date +%s) EOF mv \"$prom.$$\" \"$prom\" # atomic cutover Writing three numbers atomically into the Prometheus textfile collector via a temporary file and mv enables two critical alerts: non-zero exit codes and stale timestamps ( time() - backup_last_run_timestamp > 90000). And the counter-rule of SRE: a script that silently restarts a failing service without recording metrics or raising alerts is harmful - it masks memory leaks and progressive degradation until restarts no longer recover the service. What gets asked: “How do you monitor background shell script execution, and why is silent service auto-restarting dangerous?”"],["09.html#artifacts","Artifacts: what you take away from this page","Practices SRE","Artifacts: what you take away from this page Reading >> Your life quantum · Dignity partnership with AI · Rewiring your neural network A triage route and safeguards for automation. Your time was well spent if during incident triage you inspect unlinked open file descriptors and inode saturation before restarting services, and automatically equip every background script with non-blocking flock, ionice, and timestamp metrics. That is the essence of rewiring: scripts cease being invisible to monitoring and dangerous to neighboring processes. First-minute triage sequence: disk and inodes, memory, ports, unit status, journal, heavy processes. Three kernel traps: lsof +L1 and df -i when diverging from du, OOM in dmesg, and zombies in status Z. Non-destructive log inspection: grep -h, --line-buffered, and streaming zgrep without disk extraction. Workload isolation: non-blocking flock -n against overlap, nice / ionice -c3 for I/O pacing, and verifying scheduler compliance. Storm prevention: randomized startup jitter and exponential backoff + jitter. Script metrics: atomic emission of exit code, duration, and timestamp via mv."],["10.html#first-script","1 · Your first script: file, permissions, execution","Examples · Noob","1 · Your first script: file, permissions, execution Task: Turn two commands into an executable program. #!/usr/bin/env bash echo \"today: $(date +%F)\" df -h / | tail -1 chmod +x hello.sh # once./hello.sh # execute bash hello.sh # without execute permission Why this works: A script file is ordinary text; the permission bit makes it executable, not the file extension. The./ prefix is mandatory: without it, the shell searches PATH, which does not include the current directory - resulting in “command not found” for a file sitting right beside you. bash hello.sh works without execute permissions because bash itself executes, taking the script as a file argument."],["10.html#quoting","2 · Variables and quotes","Examples · Noob","2 · Variables and quotes Task: Output a filename containing a space. file=\"my report.txt\" echo $file # my report.txt - lucky ls $file # ERROR: two files ls \"$file\" # correct rm -f $file # deletes \"my\" and \"report.txt\" Why this works: Bash expands the variable value and then splits it on whitespace. echo masks this because it prints all arguments separated by spaces - creating a false impression that everything worked. Quotes prevent word splitting. The rule is simple: unquoted expansion must be an intentional decision, never the default."],["10.html#conditionals","3 · Conditions: check before acting","Examples · Noob","3 · Conditions: check before acting Task: Copy a file if it exists; otherwise clearly report the failure. src=\"data.csv\" if [ -f \"$src\" ]; then cp \"$src\" backup/ else echo \"missing file: $src\" >&2 exit 1 fi Why this works: [ -f ] checks for an existing regular file; -d tests for a directory, -r for readability, and -z for an empty string. Error messages must go to >&2 - to stderr, not standard output. And exiting with a non-zero code is mandatory: without it, callers assume the script succeeded."],["10.html#file-loop","4 · Looping over files (and why not to use ls)","Examples · Noob","4 · Looping over files (and why not to use ls) Task: Process all.log files in a directory. # correct for f in *.log; do [ -e \"$f\" ] || continue # none found echo \"processing: $f\" done # incorrect, yet common for f in $(ls *.log); do echo \"processing: $f\" done Why this works: The glob pattern *.log is expanded directly by the shell, preserving full filenames intact - even with embedded spaces. $(ls) outputs a single string that is subjected to word splitting, turning “old file.log” into two non-existent paths. The check [ -e \"$f\" ] is essential: if no files match, the glob remains as a literal string, causing the loop to run once with the literal string *.log."],["10.html#arguments","5 · Arguments and exit status","Examples · Noob","5 · Arguments and exit status Task: Accept a name from the user and report whether the operation succeeded. #!/usr/bin/env bash name=\"$1\" if [ -z \"$name\" ]; then echo \"usage: $0 <name>\" >&2 exit 2 fi grep -q \"$name\" users.txt echo \"exit code: $?\" Why this works: $1 is the first positional argument, $# is the argument count, and $0 is the script name. $? holds the exit status of the most recently executed command, serving as the sole mechanism to determine whether a command succeeded: grep returns 0 on match and 1 on no match. Zero signifies success; any non-zero value indicates failure. Exit code 2 conventionally denotes invalid invocation."],["10.html#reading-files","6 · Reading files line by line","Examples · Noob","6 · Reading files line by line Task: Iterate through the lines of a file and process each. while IFS= read -r line; do echo \"line: $line\" done < names.txt Why this works: Three necessary details. IFS= before read prevents stripping leading and trailing whitespace. -r disables backslash escape interpretation - without it, C:\\new turns into a string with an embedded newline. And the input redirection < file belongs after done, not before while."],["10.html#artifacts","Artifacts: what you take away from this page","Examples · Noob","Artifacts: what you take away from this page Reading >> Your life quantum · Dignity partnership with AI · Rewiring your neural network Six explained principles instead of six memorized recipes. Your time was well spent if you now understand why./ is mandatory, why quotes are not optional decoration, and why $(ls) in a loop is broken. That is the essence of rewiring: unfamiliar scripts stop looking like cryptic incantations and become readable code. Permissions and./ - why an adjacent file shows “not found”. Quoting as protection against word splitting. Pre-action checks and exiting with non-zero status codes. Glob looping instead of parsing ls. $1, $#, $? - arguments and exit status. while IFS= read -r and why each piece is necessary."],["11.html#trap-err","1 · Failing loudly: trap ERR with line numbers","Examples · Pro","1 · Failing loudly: trap ERR with line numbers Task: Ensure a failing script automatically reports where the failure occurred. #!/usr/bin/env bash set -Eeuo pipefail on_err() { local rc=$? echo \"FAILURE: line $1, exit code $rc\" >&2 exit \"$rc\" } trap 'on_err $LINENO' ERR Why this works: Bash lacks stack traces on failure, so the failure line number is the sole substitute for a trace. The -E option is mandatory: without it, the ERR trap is not inherited by shell functions or subshells, causing errors inside functions to bypass the handler. local rc=$? must be captured on the very first line: any subsequent command overwrites the exit status."],["11.html#mktemp","2 · Temporary directory guaranteed to clean up","Examples · Pro","2 · Temporary directory guaranteed to clean up Task: Process intermediate files leaving zero debris on error or Ctrl+C. tmp=$(mktemp -d \"${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/app.XXXXXX\") cleanup() { rm -rf \"$tmp\"; } trap cleanup EXIT INT TERM work > \"$tmp/out\" mv \"$tmp/out\" result.txt Why this works: The EXIT pseudo-signal trap runs on any script termination, including set -e failures - with the sole exception of SIGKILL, which the kernel prohibits trapping. INT and TERM add handling for terminal interruptions and process termination signals. Let mktemp generate unique names: predictable filenames in shared /tmp create race conditions under concurrent runs and open symlink attack vulnerabilities. Atomic renaming into final placement ensures callers never observe a partially written file."],["11.html#getopts","3 · Command options and mandatory --dry-run","Examples · Pro","3 · Command options and mandatory --dry-run Task: Equip a script with options and a “show what would happen without executing” mode. dry=0; verbose=0 while getopts \":nvh\" opt; do case $opt in n) dry=1;; v) verbose=1;; h) usage; exit 0;; \\?) echo \"unknown option\" >&2; exit 2;; esac done shift $((OPTIND - 1)) run() { (( dry)) && { echo \"DRY: $*\"; return 0; }; \"$@\"; } run rm -rf \"$target\" Why this works: A leading colon in the option specification string puts getopts into silent mode, giving you control over error reporting. shift $((OPTIND - 1)) consumes parsed options, leaving $1 pointing to the first positional argument. The run wrapper makes dry-run support architectural rather than an afterthought: all mutating commands pass through it, making omitting --dry-run harder than remembering it. One critical caveat: shell redirections are evaluated before entering a function. run echo x > file truncates file even in dry-run mode because > has already executed. Encapsulate file writing inside the command: run sh -c 'echo x > file' or write through a dedicated helper function that performs its own dry-run check."],["11.html#retry","4 · Retries with exponential backoff","Examples · Pro","4 · Retries with exponential backoff Task: Survive transient network hiccups without degenerating into an infinite loop. retry() { local n=0 max=5 delay=1 until \"$@\"; do n=$((n + 1)) (( n >= max)) && return 1 sleep \"$delay\" delay=$((delay * 2)) done } retry curl -fsS --max-time 10 \"$url\" Why this works: Three conditions distinguish a resilient retry from an infinite hang: an attempt ceiling, growing backoff delays, and a command-level timeout. Without --max-time, retries are useless: the very first request can stall indefinitely. curl -f is mandatory: without it, HTTP 500 errors are treated as successful exits, leaving nothing to retry."],["11.html#parallel","5 · Bounded parallel processing with error aggregation","Examples · Pro","5 · Bounded parallel processing with error aggregation Task: Process a hundred files across four parallel workers and verify if any failed. export -f process # visible in xargs printf '%s\\0' logs/*.log \\ | xargs -0 -P4 -I{} bash -c 'process \"$@\"' _ {} \\ || echo \"some tasks failed\" >&2 # manual tracking with exit codes: pids=(); for f in logs/*.log; do work \"$f\" & pids+=($!); done rc=0; for p in \"${pids[@]}\"; do wait \"$p\" || rc=1; done Why this works: The null delimiter \\0 paired with -0 immunizes the pipeline against filenames with spaces and newlines. xargs returns a non-zero exit status if any sub-process fails - providing the aggregated error detection absent in naive & plus wait workflows. If you need individual process exit codes, store $! in an array and wait on each PID individually."],["11.html#flock","6 · Single instance: protecting against concurrent runs","Examples · Pro","6 · Single instance: protecting against concurrent runs Task: Prevent a cron job from starting a second instance if the previous run is still active. exec 9>/var/lock/app.lock flock -n 9 || { echo \"already running\" >&2; exit 0; } # subsequent standard execution Why this works: The lock is bound to an open file descriptor, not a filesystem path: it is released automatically when the process terminates - including abrupt crashes. A custom “pid-file / flag-file” lacks this property: upon an unhandled crash, stale lock files remain on disk blocking future runs permanently. The -n flag requests non-blocking acquisition; exiting with status 0 is deliberate here - for cron, an already running instance is an expected condition, not a failure."],["11.html#log-levels","7 · Leveled logging","Examples · Pro","7 · Leveled logging Task: Provide diagnostic logging that does not corrupt command stdout. LOG_LEVEL=\"${LOG_LEVEL:-info}\" _log() { local lvl=$1; shift printf '%s [%s] %s\\n' \\ \"$(date -Is)\" \"$lvl\" \"$*\" >&2 } info() { [ \"$LOG_LEVEL\" = debug ] || [ \"$LOG_LEVEL\" = info ] && _log INFO \"$@\"; } die() { _log ERROR \"$@\"; exit 1; } Why this works: All log output is directed to stderr because stdout is reserved for data consumed by downstream pipeline stages. Timestamps formatted with -Is (ISO 8601) sort naturally as strings and parse unambiguously by machine tools. Log level is driven by an environment variable: verbosity can be toggled without editing script code - essential when triaging issues in production."],["11.html#artifacts","Artifacts: what you take away from this page","Examples · Pro","Artifacts: what you take away from this page Reading >> Your life quantum · Dignity partnership with AI · Rewiring your neural network Seven modular patterns and the exact failures each defends against. Your time was well spent if when drafting your next script you reflexively ask “what happens if this crashes midway through” - and already know the solution. That is the essence of rewiring: failure handling stops being an afterthought and becomes an integral part of design. set -Eeuo pipefail and trap ERR $LINENO - failures name their exact location. mktemp -d with trap EXIT INT TERM - guaranteed cleanup on all exits except kill -9. getopts and the run wrapper - architectural dry-run checks and internal redirection. Bounded retries with timeouts, and why curl -f is mandatory. xargs -0 -P - safe filenames and aggregated exit status reporting. Descriptor-based flock instead of brittle pid-files. Stderr logging with environment-driven log levels."],["12.html#no-script","1 · The decision not to write a script","Examples · IGL","1 · The decision not to write a script Situation: The team requests a “quick script” to monitor state and reconcile drift. # what is requested: # - inspect what is deployed # - diff against desired state # - converge to desired state # - record actions taken # this describes Terraform, # not a shell script Why this matters: The four requirements above define a state management tool. Any shell script implementing them will inevitably accumulate custom state storage, bespoke diff algorithms, and distinct failure modes. The IGL decision here is not writing a cleaner script, but calling the task by its true name and delegating it to an established tool where reconciliation logic is already hardened. The costliest script is the one that successfully solves the wrong problem. Decision cost: Declining costs a single conversation and perhaps a week evaluating a dedicated tool. Agreeing commits to indefinite maintenance of an in-house state management engine - with costs scaling directly with managed resource count."],["12.html#tool-contract","2 · Tool contract: scripts for machines","Examples · IGL","2 · Tool contract: scripts for machines Situation: Your script will be invoked by downstream scripts, CI pipelines, and automated agents - machines rather than humans. # contract to declare explicitly: # stdout - results only # stderr - all diagnostics and logs # 0 - success # 1 - expected domain failure # 2 - invalid command invocation # 3-63 - custom codes, documented in --help # 126, 127, 128+N - reserved by shell # --json - machine-readable output # --dry-run - non-destructive execution Why this matters: For a machine-facing tool, interface contracts supersede internal implementation: implementations can be refactored freely, but breaking an established contract breaks dependent automation. Exit codes must distinguish “not found” from “crashed”: callers can handle the former cleanly, while the latter must halt pipelines. Providing a dedicated --json flag allows iterating on human-readable text without disrupting parsers; without it, any formatting polish is a breaking API change. For standardized exit code conventions, reference sysexits.h: 64 for command line syntax errors, 70 for internal software bugs, 75 for transient failures suitable for retries. Never assign custom meanings to 126, 127, or 128+N: these are emitted by the shell itself, and callers will be unable to distinguish your error code from “permission denied”, “command not found”, or “terminated by signal”. Decision cost: Formalizing the contract costs thirty minutes and a dozen lines in --help. Omitting it turns every future output edit into an outage for callers you may not even know exist."],["12.html#retry-safety","3 · Idempotence and retry resilience in distributed environments","Examples · IGL","3 · Idempotence and retry resilience in distributed environments Situation: A job is triggered by a scheduler that upon hitting a timeout fires a retry without waiting for the prior run to complete. # not idempotent: echo \"$line\" >> report.txt counter=$((counter + 1)) # idempotent: key=\"run-$(date +%F)-$id\" grep -q \"^$key\" done.log || { work \"$id\" echo \"$key\" >> done.log } Why this matters: In distributed environments, retries are expected operational reality, not edge cases: network timeouts, node rescheduling, and duplicate webhook events occur constantly. Resilience means ensuring duplicate invocations do not corrupt state. The architectural decision is introducing an idempotency key and conditioning mutations upon it. Note the architectural boundary: grep followed by echo is not atomic; true concurrency requires filesystem locking or external transactional storage - signaling that the task has outgrown plain shell scripts. Decision cost: An idempotency key and completion log require ten lines. Without them, scheduler retries produce duplicate billings, duplicate notification blasts, or destructive repeated deletions."],["12.html#observability","4 · Observability: what scripts must emit","Examples · IGL","4 · Observability: what scripts must emit Situation: A script runs unattended overnight, and its outcome is only reviewed twenty-four hours later. start=$(date +%s) trap 'report \"$?\" \"$start\"' EXIT report() { local rc=$1 t=$(( $(date +%s) - $2)) printf 'result=%s rc=%s sec=%s items=%s\\n' \\ \"$([ \"$rc\" = 0 ] && echo ok || echo fail)\" \\ \"$rc\" \"$t\" \"$count\" >&2 } Why this matters: An uninstrumented script is only noticeable when it crashes outright - and only if someone is watching. A minimum telemetry payload of four metrics - outcome, exit code, elapsed duration, and processed volume - turns it into an observable component: metrics can be trended, revealing performance degradation long before catastrophic outage. Structured key=value lines parse natively across log aggregators without complex regular expressions. Emitting telemetry from an EXIT trap guarantees reports even during unhandled script crashes. Decision cost: One structured line emitted per execution. Without it, performance degradation remains hidden until complete failure occurs - usually discovered by customers first."],["12.html#blast-radius","5 · Blast radius: bounding worst-case failure modes","Examples · IGL","5 · Blast radius: bounding worst-case failure modes Situation: A script purges aged resources. The question is not “does it work,” but “what happens when its logic fails.” # restrict execution outside allowed scope case \"$target\" in /opt/app/releases/*);; *) echo \"out of scope: $target\" >&2; exit 2;; esac # cap work per single invocation if (( ${#list[@]} > 20)); then echo \"too many items\" >&2 exit 2 fi Why this matters: Two safety guards outweigh any amount of careful coding. Scope whitelisting prevents mutations outside designated filesystem trees - even under empty variables or regex evaluation bugs. Per-run batch limits downgrade potential catastrophes into routine operational tickets: a script that resolves three thousand purge candidates instead of five aborts immediately and demands human review. This is designing for the worst-case scenario rather than the happy path. Decision cost: Two validation checks at script initialization. Without them, a single filtering typo risks destroying everything within the script's ambient IAM permissions."],["12.html#decommission","6 · Decommissioning scripts gracefully","Examples · IGL","6 · Decommissioning scripts gracefully Situation: Legacy script functionality has migrated into a platform tool, but dependent callers continue invoking the original entry point. #!/usr/bin/env bash echo \"DEPRECATED: use 'app deploy'\" >&2 echo \"this script will be decommissioned 2026-12-01\" >&2 exec app deploy \"$@\" # proxying for now Why this matters: You cannot delete a shared script overnight: dependent CI jobs, external cron schedules, and muscle memory remain active across teams. A proxying deprecation phase with a definitive sunset date affords calling teams time to migrate while surfacing remaining consumers in audit logs. Specific sunset dates are essential: open-ended “deprecated” warnings are ignored indefinitely. This operational discipline is what elevates software engineering above raw scripting. Decision cost: A month of proxying and reviewing invocation logs. Skipping this step guarantees breaking uncataloged dependencies on the day of removal."],["12.html#artifacts","Artifacts: what you take away from this page","Examples · IGL","Artifacts: what you take away from this page Reading >> Your life quantum · Dignity partnership with AI · Rewiring your neural network Six architectural decisions evaluated before code is written. Your time was well spent if before approving your next script you ask “what happens when its filter fails” and “who will decommission this when it is superseded.” That is the essence of rewiring: accountability shifts from immediate functionality to long-term blast radius, defining the boundary between an implementer and a technical lead. Signs of misplaced scope: inspect, diff, converge, record - this belongs in state engines. Tool contracts: dedicated streams, exit code semantics, --json, --dry-run - avoiding shell reserved codes 126, 127, and 128+N. Idempotency keys to survive distributed scheduler retries. Four core telemetry metrics: outcome status, exit code, duration, processed count. Scope whitelists and batch caps: bounding failure blast radius. Dated proxying phases when deprecating and retiring scripts."],["13.html#running-files","Execution, files, and paths (1–10)","Questions · Noob","Execution, files, and paths (1–10) What does the first line #!/usr/bin/env bash do? It informs the kernel which interpreter to use when executing the file. The env form searches for Bash across PATH, rather than hardcoding /bin/bash. Why this gets asked: tests whether you understand that a script is an ordinary file rather than a distinct file type. Why does script.sh fail to run, while./script.sh executes? Without./, the shell searches directories listed in PATH, which does not include the current directory. Why this gets asked: the most common initial beginner error; verifies understanding of PATH. What is the purpose of chmod +x? It sets the execute permission bit. Without it, the file can only be run via bash script.sh. Why this gets asked: distinguishes file existence from execution privileges. How does./script.sh differ from bash script.sh? In the first case, the interpreter is determined by the shebang and execute permissions are required; in the second, bash itself executes with the script as an argument. Why this gets asked: verifies understanding of process spawning and interpretation. How does. (dot) differ from./script.sh?. script.sh or source executes the file within the current shell environment, preserving its variables;./script.sh spawns a separate child process. Why this gets asked: explains why cd inside a script does not change the caller's terminal directory. What is PATH? A colon-delimited list of directories where the shell searches for executable programs. Why this gets asked: fundamental concept; required to troubleshoot “command not found”. How do you determine which program will execute for a given command name? type cmd or command -v cmd: displays the path, alias, function, or shell builtin. Why this gets asked: verifies whether you diagnose root causes rather than guess. How does a script locate its own containing directory? dir=$(cd \"$(dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")\" && pwd). The current working directory is where the script was invoked from, not where it resides, and relative paths inside evaluate against the former. Why this gets asked: the most frequent practical question when writing production-ready scripts. How does an absolute path differ from a relative path? An absolute path starts with / and is independent of working directory; a relative path resolves relative to the current working directory. Why this gets asked: scripts almost always require absolute paths or explicit anchors. How do you view command documentation? man cmd for system binaries, help cmd for Bash builtins, cmd --help for most standard utilities. Why this gets asked: checks whether you know that shell builtins do not appear in man."],["13.html#running-files","What does the first line #!/usr/bin/env bash do?","Questions · Noob","What does the first line #!/usr/bin/env bash do? It informs the kernel which interpreter to use when executing the file. The env form searches for Bash across PATH, rather than hardcoding /bin/bash. Why this gets asked: tests whether you understand that a script is an ordinary file rather than a distinct file type."],["13.html#running-files","Why does script.sh fail to run, while./script.sh executes?","Questions · Noob","Why does script.sh fail to run, while./script.sh executes? Without./, the shell searches directories listed in PATH, which does not include the current directory. Why this gets asked: the most common initial beginner error; verifies understanding of PATH."],["13.html#running-files","What is the purpose of chmod +x?","Questions · Noob","What is the purpose of chmod +x? It sets the execute permission bit. Without it, the file can only be run via bash script.sh. Why this gets asked: distinguishes file existence from execution privileges."],["13.html#running-files","How does./script.sh differ from bash script.sh?","Questions · Noob","How does./script.sh differ from bash script.sh? In the first case, the interpreter is determined by the shebang and execute permissions are required; in the second, bash itself executes with the script as an argument. Why this gets asked: verifies understanding of process spawning and interpretation."],["13.html#running-files","How does. (dot) differ from./script.sh?","Questions · Noob","How does. (dot) differ from./script.sh?. script.sh or source executes the file within the current shell environment, preserving its variables;./script.sh spawns a separate child process. Why this gets asked: explains why cd inside a script does not change the caller's terminal directory."],["13.html#running-files","What is PATH?","Questions · Noob","What is PATH? A colon-delimited list of directories where the shell searches for executable programs. Why this gets asked: fundamental concept; required to troubleshoot “command not found”."],["13.html#running-files","How do you determine which program will execute for a given command name?","Questions · Noob","How do you determine which program will execute for a given command name? type cmd or command -v cmd: displays the path, alias, function, or shell builtin. Why this gets asked: verifies whether you diagnose root causes rather than guess."],["13.html#running-files","How does a script locate its own containing directory?","Questions · Noob","How does a script locate its own containing directory? dir=$(cd \"$(dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")\" && pwd). The current working directory is where the script was invoked from, not where it resides, and relative paths inside evaluate against the former. Why this gets asked: the most frequent practical question when writing production-ready scripts."],["13.html#running-files","How does an absolute path differ from a relative path?","Questions · Noob","How does an absolute path differ from a relative path? An absolute path starts with / and is independent of working directory; a relative path resolves relative to the current working directory. Why this gets asked: scripts almost always require absolute paths or explicit anchors."],["13.html#running-files","How do you view command documentation?","Questions · Noob","How do you view command documentation? man cmd for system binaries, help cmd for Bash builtins, cmd --help for most standard utilities. Why this gets asked: checks whether you know that shell builtins do not appear in man."],["13.html#variables-quoting","Variables and quoting (11–20)","Questions · Noob","Variables and quoting (11–20) How do you declare a variable and what characters must not surround the assignment? name=value, with no spaces around =. Adding a space causes the shell to interpret the variable name as a command name. Why this gets asked: classic beginner syntax mistake, immediately apparent. Why must variables be enclosed in quotes like \"$var\"? Without quotes, the value undergoes word splitting on whitespace/newlines and pathname expansion. A path with spaces fragments into multiple arguments, and *.log expands into directory listings. Why this gets asked: the leading cause of bugs and security vulnerabilities on production data. How do double quotes differ from single quotes? Double quotes allow parameter and command expansions; single quotes preserve text literally. Why this gets asked: tests understanding of when expansions occur. What does ${var:-default} evaluate to? Default value expansion: if the variable is unset or null, it evaluates to default. Why this gets asked: the most common idiomatic pattern to prevent crashes on empty variables. How does $var differ from ${var}? Braces disambiguate variable names from adjacent characters ( ${var}_x), and are mandatory for positional parameters beyond nine: $10 evaluates as $1 followed by literal 0, whereas the tenth parameter is ${10}. Why this gets asked: checks whether you understand where token identifiers terminate. What is command substitution? $(cmd) captures the standard output of a command and substitutes it into the string. Backticks are deprecated: nesting them requires cumbersome escaping. Why this gets asked: the foundation of computing dynamic inputs in shell scripting. What does $? store? The exit status of the most recently executed command: 0 represents success, non-zero represents error. Why this gets asked: prerequisite for control flow and decision-making on results. What do $1, $@, and $# represent? The first positional argument, all positional arguments, and the total argument count. Why this gets asked: foundational toolkit for parametrized scripts. How do you make an environment variable available to child processes? export VAR=value. Without export, the variable remains private to the current shell process. Why this gets asked: explains why child binaries fail to see shell-assigned variables. What is an alias and why does it not work inside scripts? A command shortcut for interactive shells. By default, aliases are not expanded in non-interactive scripts, and scripts do not source.bashrc where aliases are typically declared. Why this gets asked: verifies understanding of the boundary between interactive and scripted shells."],["13.html#variables-quoting","How do you declare a variable and what characters must not surround the assignment?","Questions · Noob","How do you declare a variable and what characters must not surround the assignment? name=value, with no spaces around =. Adding a space causes the shell to interpret the variable name as a command name. Why this gets asked: classic beginner syntax mistake, immediately apparent."],["13.html#variables-quoting","Why must variables be enclosed in quotes like \"$var\"?","Questions · Noob","Why must variables be enclosed in quotes like \"$var\"? Without quotes, the value undergoes word splitting on whitespace/newlines and pathname expansion. A path with spaces fragments into multiple arguments, and *.log expands into directory listings. Why this gets asked: the leading cause of bugs and security vulnerabilities on production data."],["13.html#variables-quoting","How do double quotes differ from single quotes?","Questions · Noob","How do double quotes differ from single quotes? Double quotes allow parameter and command expansions; single quotes preserve text literally. Why this gets asked: tests understanding of when expansions occur."],["13.html#variables-quoting","What does ${var:-default} evaluate to?","Questions · Noob","What does ${var:-default} evaluate to? Default value expansion: if the variable is unset or null, it evaluates to default. Why this gets asked: the most common idiomatic pattern to prevent crashes on empty variables."],["13.html#variables-quoting","How does $var differ from ${var}?","Questions · Noob","How does $var differ from ${var}? Braces disambiguate variable names from adjacent characters ( ${var}_x), and are mandatory for positional parameters beyond nine: $10 evaluates as $1 followed by literal 0, whereas the tenth parameter is ${10}. Why this gets asked: checks whether you understand where token identifiers terminate."],["13.html#variables-quoting","What is command substitution?","Questions · Noob","What is command substitution? $(cmd) captures the standard output of a command and substitutes it into the string. Backticks are deprecated: nesting them requires cumbersome escaping. Why this gets asked: the foundation of computing dynamic inputs in shell scripting."],["13.html#variables-quoting","What does $? store?","Questions · Noob","What does $? store? The exit status of the most recently executed command: 0 represents success, non-zero represents error. Why this gets asked: prerequisite for control flow and decision-making on results."],["13.html#variables-quoting","What do $1, $@, and $# represent?","Questions · Noob","What do $1, $@, and $# represent? The first positional argument, all positional arguments, and the total argument count. Why this gets asked: foundational toolkit for parametrized scripts."],["13.html#variables-quoting","How do you make an environment variable available to child processes?","Questions · Noob","How do you make an environment variable available to child processes? export VAR=value. Without export, the variable remains private to the current shell process. Why this gets asked: explains why child binaries fail to see shell-assigned variables."],["13.html#variables-quoting","What is an alias and why does it not work inside scripts?","Questions · Noob","What is an alias and why does it not work inside scripts? A command shortcut for interactive shells. By default, aliases are not expanded in non-interactive scripts, and scripts do not source.bashrc where aliases are typically declared. Why this gets asked: verifies understanding of the boundary between interactive and scripted shells."],["13.html#conditions-loops","Conditions and loops (21–30)","Questions · Noob","Conditions and loops (21–30) How do you test if a file exists? if [ -f \"$f\" ]; then …. Use -d for directories, and -e for any filesystem object. Why this gets asked: foundational pre-action validation pattern. What do the -z and -n operators check? -z checks if a string is empty; -n checks if a string is non-empty. Why this gets asked: argument validation almost always begins here. How does [ ] differ from [[ ]]? [ is a standard builtin command whose arguments undergo word splitting and globbing. [[ is a shell keyword with custom parsing: safe with empty strings, supports pattern matching, &&, and =~. Only [ is portable across all POSIX shells. Why this gets asked: classic interview question testing portability and parser mechanics. How do you compare numbers rather than strings? Use [ \"$a\" -gt \"$b\" ] or arithmetic evaluation (( a > b)). The > character inside [ ] acts as a redirection operator. Why this gets asked: an insidious trap that silently corrupts files. How do you iterate over files in a directory? for f in *.log; do … done - glob expansion is performed natively by the shell. Why this gets asked: checks whether you avoid the anti-pattern of parsing $(ls). Why should you never write for f in $(ls)? The output of ls is a raw string subjected to word splitting, breaking filenames containing spaces. Why this gets asked: textbook mistake, fails on the first encounter with production data. How do you read a file line by line? while IFS= read -r line; do … done < file. Why this gets asked: tests understanding of raw reading ( -r) and whitespace preservation ( IFS=). How do you trace what a script is actually executing? Run bash -x script.sh or set set -x inside: every command is printed after expansions, prefixed with +. This is vastly faster than scattering manual echo statements. Why this gets asked: essential debugging skill; without it engineers debug blindly. How do you implement branching across multiple pattern choices? case \"$x\" in a) …;; b) …;; *) …;; esac - performs glob pattern matching. Why this gets asked: clean alternative to verbose if-elif chains. What do || and && signify between commands? && executes the right-hand command if the left succeeded (returned 0); || executes if it failed (returned non-zero). Why this gets asked: foundation of idiomatic one-liner checks and fallbacks."],["13.html#conditions-loops","How do you test if a file exists?","Questions · Noob","How do you test if a file exists? if [ -f \"$f\" ]; then …. Use -d for directories, and -e for any filesystem object. Why this gets asked: foundational pre-action validation pattern."],["13.html#conditions-loops","What do the -z and -n operators check?","Questions · Noob","What do the -z and -n operators check? -z checks if a string is empty; -n checks if a string is non-empty. Why this gets asked: argument validation almost always begins here."],["13.html#conditions-loops","How does [ ] differ from [[ ]]?","Questions · Noob","How does [ ] differ from [[ ]]? [ is a standard builtin command whose arguments undergo word splitting and globbing. [[ is a shell keyword with custom parsing: safe with empty strings, supports pattern matching, &&, and =~. Only [ is portable across all POSIX shells. Why this gets asked: classic interview question testing portability and parser mechanics."],["13.html#conditions-loops","How do you compare numbers rather than strings?","Questions · Noob","How do you compare numbers rather than strings? Use [ \"$a\" -gt \"$b\" ] or arithmetic evaluation (( a > b)). The > character inside [ ] acts as a redirection operator. Why this gets asked: an insidious trap that silently corrupts files."],["13.html#conditions-loops","How do you iterate over files in a directory?","Questions · Noob","How do you iterate over files in a directory? for f in *.log; do … done - glob expansion is performed natively by the shell. Why this gets asked: checks whether you avoid the anti-pattern of parsing $(ls)."],["13.html#conditions-loops","Why should you never write for f in $(ls)?","Questions · Noob","Why should you never write for f in $(ls)? The output of ls is a raw string subjected to word splitting, breaking filenames containing spaces. Why this gets asked: textbook mistake, fails on the first encounter with production data."],["13.html#conditions-loops","How do you read a file line by line?","Questions · Noob","How do you read a file line by line? while IFS= read -r line; do … done < file. Why this gets asked: tests understanding of raw reading ( -r) and whitespace preservation ( IFS=)."],["13.html#conditions-loops","How do you trace what a script is actually executing?","Questions · Noob","How do you trace what a script is actually executing? Run bash -x script.sh or set set -x inside: every command is printed after expansions, prefixed with +. This is vastly faster than scattering manual echo statements. Why this gets asked: essential debugging skill; without it engineers debug blindly."],["13.html#conditions-loops","How do you implement branching across multiple pattern choices?","Questions · Noob","How do you implement branching across multiple pattern choices? case \"$x\" in a) …;; b) …;; *) …;; esac - performs glob pattern matching. Why this gets asked: clean alternative to verbose if-elif chains."],["13.html#conditions-loops","What do || and && signify between commands?","Questions · Noob","What do || and && signify between commands? && executes the right-hand command if the left succeeded (returned 0); || executes if it failed (returned non-zero). Why this gets asked: foundation of idiomatic one-liner checks and fallbacks."],["13.html#text-pipelines","Files, text, and pipelines (31–40)","Questions · Noob","Files, text, and pipelines (31–40) How do you find files by name across nested directories? find. -name '*.log' -type f. Why this gets asked: universal administrative command asked in every interview. How does grep differ from find? grep searches text contents inside files, while find locates filesystem entries by attributes. Why this gets asked: checks whether you conflate filesystem inspection with text parsing. What do the -i, -r, and -v flags do in grep? Case-insensitive matching, recursive directory search, and inverted match (select non-matching lines). Why this gets asked: the three most frequently utilized grep flags. How do you inspect the head and tail of a file? head -20 f and tail -20 f; tail -f streams real-time appended data. Why this gets asked: daily routine in log inspection and troubleshooting. What does 2>/dev/null do? Redirects standard error to the null device. Convenient, but dangerous: it suppresses diagnostic error messages needed to debug failures. Why this gets asked: appears frequently; tests whether you understand the hidden cost. How do you replace text in a file in place? sed -i 's/old/new/g' file. On macOS BSD sed, -i requires an explicit backup extension argument: -i ''. Why this gets asked: tests awareness of cross-platform differences between GNU and BSD tooling. How do you sort text and remove duplicate lines? sort file | uniq; uniq -c prefixes occurrence counts. Because uniq only collapses adjacent duplicate lines, pre-sorting is mandatory. Why this gets asked: classic mistake - running uniq without sorting first. What is a pipeline |? An inter-process communication stream: standard output of the left command is connected to standard input of the right; both execute concurrently. Why this gets asked: core pillar of Unix philosophy. How does > differ from >>? > truncates and overwrites the target file, while >> appends data to the end. Why this gets asked: one of the costliest single-character typos in systems administration. How do you direct both stdout and stderr into the same file? cmd > file 2>&1 - evaluation order from left to right is critical. Why this gets asked: verifies understanding of file descriptor duplication."],["13.html#text-pipelines","How do you find files by name across nested directories?","Questions · Noob","How do you find files by name across nested directories? find. -name '*.log' -type f. Why this gets asked: universal administrative command asked in every interview."],["13.html#text-pipelines","How does grep differ from find?","Questions · Noob","How does grep differ from find? grep searches text contents inside files, while find locates filesystem entries by attributes. Why this gets asked: checks whether you conflate filesystem inspection with text parsing."],["13.html#text-pipelines","What do the -i, -r, and -v flags do in grep?","Questions · Noob","What do the -i, -r, and -v flags do in grep? Case-insensitive matching, recursive directory search, and inverted match (select non-matching lines). Why this gets asked: the three most frequently utilized grep flags."],["13.html#text-pipelines","How do you inspect the head and tail of a file?","Questions · Noob","How do you inspect the head and tail of a file? head -20 f and tail -20 f; tail -f streams real-time appended data. Why this gets asked: daily routine in log inspection and troubleshooting."],["13.html#text-pipelines","What does 2>/dev/null do?","Questions · Noob","What does 2>/dev/null do? Redirects standard error to the null device. Convenient, but dangerous: it suppresses diagnostic error messages needed to debug failures. Why this gets asked: appears frequently; tests whether you understand the hidden cost."],["13.html#text-pipelines","How do you replace text in a file in place?","Questions · Noob","How do you replace text in a file in place? sed -i 's/old/new/g' file. On macOS BSD sed, -i requires an explicit backup extension argument: -i ''. Why this gets asked: tests awareness of cross-platform differences between GNU and BSD tooling."],["13.html#text-pipelines","How do you sort text and remove duplicate lines?","Questions · Noob","How do you sort text and remove duplicate lines? sort file | uniq; uniq -c prefixes occurrence counts. Because uniq only collapses adjacent duplicate lines, pre-sorting is mandatory. Why this gets asked: classic mistake - running uniq without sorting first."],["13.html#text-pipelines","What is a pipeline |?","Questions · Noob","What is a pipeline |? An inter-process communication stream: standard output of the left command is connected to standard input of the right; both execute concurrently. Why this gets asked: core pillar of Unix philosophy."],["13.html#text-pipelines","How does > differ from >>?","Questions · Noob","How does > differ from >>? > truncates and overwrites the target file, while >> appends data to the end. Why this gets asked: one of the costliest single-character typos in systems administration."],["13.html#text-pipelines","How do you direct both stdout and stderr into the same file?","Questions · Noob","How do you direct both stdout and stderr into the same file? cmd > file 2>&1 - evaluation order from left to right is critical. Why this gets asked: verifies understanding of file descriptor duplication."],["13.html#processes-permissions","Processes, permissions, and triage (41–50)","Questions · Noob","Processes, permissions, and triage (41–50) What are standard file descriptors 0, 1, and 2? Standard input (stdin), standard output (stdout), and standard error (stderr). Primary data streams through 1, diagnostics through 2. Why this gets asked: required to explain shell redirection and piping. How do you inspect running processes? ps aux or ps -ef; interactively via top or htop. Why this gets asked: standard daily troubleshooting routine. How do you terminate a process? kill PID transmits SIGTERM - requesting clean shutdown. kill -9 transmits SIGKILL, which cannot be caught or handled by the process. Why this gets asked: tests whether you distinguish polite termination requests from kernel termination. How does Ctrl+Z differ from Ctrl+C, and what do &, bg, and fg do? Ctrl+C sends SIGINT to terminate a command, while Ctrl+Z sends SIGTSTP to suspend it. bg resumes a suspended job in the background, fg brings it to the foreground, & launches it directly in the background, and jobs lists background tasks. Why this gets asked: beginners frequently press Ctrl+Z instead of Ctrl+C, leaving dangling suspended tasks. How do you check available disk space? df -h for filesystem capacity; du -sh * for directory usage. Why this gets asked: step one in triaging almost every production incident. What do rwx permissions signify? Read, write, and execute permissions for user owner, group, and others. chmod 755 grants full access to owner, and read/execute to group and others. Why this gets asked: baseline Linux systems knowledge. How does sudo differ from logging in directly as root? sudo executes a single command with elevated privileges while recording an audit log entry. Why this gets asked: security and auditability best practices. How do you inspect environment variables? env or printenv; individually via echo \"$VAR\". Why this gets asked: essential when diagnosing “works locally, fails in CI” disparities. What is cron? A daemon scheduler for periodic commands. Critically, it runs in a clean environment without sourcing.bashrc, resulting in a minimal PATH. Why this gets asked: perennial interview pitfall. How does a symbolic link differ from a hard link? A symbolic link ( ln -s) stores the target path as a pointer and breaks if the target is deleted; a hard link is an alternate directory entry referencing the same underlying inode and functions only within a single filesystem. Why this gets asked: one of the most frequent Linux filesystem questions."],["13.html#processes-permissions","What are standard file descriptors 0, 1, and 2?","Questions · Noob","What are standard file descriptors 0, 1, and 2? Standard input (stdin), standard output (stdout), and standard error (stderr). Primary data streams through 1, diagnostics through 2. Why this gets asked: required to explain shell redirection and piping."],["13.html#processes-permissions","How do you inspect running processes?","Questions · Noob","How do you inspect running processes? ps aux or ps -ef; interactively via top or htop. Why this gets asked: standard daily troubleshooting routine."],["13.html#processes-permissions","How do you terminate a process?","Questions · Noob","How do you terminate a process? kill PID transmits SIGTERM - requesting clean shutdown. kill -9 transmits SIGKILL, which cannot be caught or handled by the process. Why this gets asked: tests whether you distinguish polite termination requests from kernel termination."],["13.html#processes-permissions","How does Ctrl+Z differ from Ctrl+C, and what do &, bg, and fg do?","Questions · Noob","How does Ctrl+Z differ from Ctrl+C, and what do &, bg, and fg do? Ctrl+C sends SIGINT to terminate a command, while Ctrl+Z sends SIGTSTP to suspend it. bg resumes a suspended job in the background, fg brings it to the foreground, & launches it directly in the background, and jobs lists background tasks. Why this gets asked: beginners frequently press Ctrl+Z instead of Ctrl+C, leaving dangling suspended tasks."],["13.html#processes-permissions","How do you check available disk space?","Questions · Noob","How do you check available disk space? df -h for filesystem capacity; du -sh * for directory usage. Why this gets asked: step one in triaging almost every production incident."],["13.html#processes-permissions","What do rwx permissions signify?","Questions · Noob","What do rwx permissions signify? Read, write, and execute permissions for user owner, group, and others. chmod 755 grants full access to owner, and read/execute to group and others. Why this gets asked: baseline Linux systems knowledge."],["13.html#processes-permissions","How does sudo differ from logging in directly as root?","Questions · Noob","How does sudo differ from logging in directly as root? sudo executes a single command with elevated privileges while recording an audit log entry. Why this gets asked: security and auditability best practices."],["13.html#processes-permissions","How do you inspect environment variables?","Questions · Noob","How do you inspect environment variables? env or printenv; individually via echo \"$VAR\". Why this gets asked: essential when diagnosing “works locally, fails in CI” disparities."],["13.html#processes-permissions","What is cron?","Questions · Noob","What is cron? A daemon scheduler for periodic commands. Critically, it runs in a clean environment without sourcing.bashrc, resulting in a minimal PATH. Why this gets asked: perennial interview pitfall."],["13.html#processes-permissions","How does a symbolic link differ from a hard link?","Questions · Noob","How does a symbolic link differ from a hard link? A symbolic link ( ln -s) stores the target path as a pointer and breaks if the target is deleted; a hard link is an alternate directory entry referencing the same underlying inode and functions only within a single filesystem. Why this gets asked: one of the most frequent Linux filesystem questions."],["13.html#artifacts","Artifacts: what you take away from this page","Questions · Noob","Artifacts: what you take away from this page Reading >> Your life quantum · Dignity partnership with AI · Rewiring your neural network Fifty verified answers and an understanding of what lies behind each. Your time was well spent if you answer half of these questions not with a memorized recipe, but with an explanation of shell mechanics. That is the essence of rewiring: answers cease being learned phrases and become deducible from how the shell operates. Ten answers on execution and paths - PATH, shebang, permissions. Ten on variables and quoting - including ${var:-default} and $?. Ten on conditions and loops - [[ ]] versus [ ], glob iteration. Ten on text and pipelines - grep, sed, sort | uniq. Ten on system fundamentals - streams, processes, signals, permissions, cron."],["14.html#strict-mode","Strict mode, errors, and signals (1–10)","Questions · Pro","Strict mode, errors, and signals (1–10) What exactly does each option in set -euo pipefail do? -e exits on non-zero return code; -u treats unset variables as errors; -o pipefail sets the pipeline return code to that of the rightmost command to exit with a non-zero status. Why this gets asked: tests whether you understand these as three separate architectural decisions rather than a single magic incantation. Name the situations where set -e does not trigger. Inside if / while conditions, to the left of && or ||, inside functions invoked as part of a condition, in command substitutions evaluated in arguments, and in local x=$(cmd): the return code of local masks the command substitution's failure code. Why this gets asked: the quintessential reliability question; separates theoretical knowledge from production reality. Why is -E required alongside trap ERR? Without it, the ERR trap is not inherited by shell functions or subshells, allowing errors inside functions to bypass the error handler. Why this gets asked: rarely known; demonstrates hands-on debugging experience. How do you find the line number where a script failed? trap 'echo \"line $LINENO\"' ERR combined with set -E. Bash does not provide built-in stack traces. Why this gets asked: evaluates how you triage production failures. How do you obtain the exit code of a specific stage in a pipeline? From the ${PIPESTATUS[@]} array immediately following the pipeline - any subsequent command overwrites it. Why this gets asked: classic differentiator; separates those who have read man bash thoroughly. Why does a script with set -e silently exit on ((i++))? Arithmetic evaluation returns exit code 1 if the arithmetic result is 0, and post-increment i++ on i=0 yields 0. The safe form is i=$((i + 1)): variable assignments always return exit code 0. Why this gets asked: classic trap: the script crashes without a single error message on a loop counter. What does trap … EXIT do and why is it more reliable than manual cleanup? It executes upon any termination the shell can detect: normal completion, error exits, or signals. The exception is SIGKILL: under kill -9, the OOM killer, or abrupt container termination, the trap does not run and temporary files remain. Why this gets asked: checks whether you reason through all exit paths, including unpreventable termination. How does exit differ from return inside a function? return terminates the function; exit terminates the entire process. However, a function invoked via command substitution $() runs in a subshell, so its exit only terminates the subshell, leaving the parent script running. Why this gets asked: tests understanding of process isolation on the most common edge case. What does exit code 130 signify? Termination by signal: 128 + N where N is the signal number; 130 corresponds to SIGINT (e.g. Ctrl+C). Why this gets asked: demonstrates ability to read exit codes in CI logs. What is a zombie process and why does kill -9 fail to remove it? It is an already-terminated process whose exit status has not yet been collected by its parent via wait. There is nothing to kill - it is dead; it disappears once the parent waits on it or the parent terminates. In containers lacking an init system, nobody collects orphans, causing zombies on PID 1. Why this gets asked: connects signals, wait, and container PID 1 responsibilities in a single question."],["14.html#strict-mode","What exactly does each option in set -euo pipefail do?","Questions · Pro","What exactly does each option in set -euo pipefail do? -e exits on non-zero return code; -u treats unset variables as errors; -o pipefail sets the pipeline return code to that of the rightmost command to exit with a non-zero status. Why this gets asked: tests whether you understand these as three separate architectural decisions rather than a single magic incantation."],["14.html#strict-mode","Name the situations where set -e does not trigger.","Questions · Pro","Name the situations where set -e does not trigger. Inside if / while conditions, to the left of && or ||, inside functions invoked as part of a condition, in command substitutions evaluated in arguments, and in local x=$(cmd): the return code of local masks the command substitution's failure code. Why this gets asked: the quintessential reliability question; separates theoretical knowledge from production reality."],["14.html#strict-mode","Why is -E required alongside trap ERR?","Questions · Pro","Why is -E required alongside trap ERR? Without it, the ERR trap is not inherited by shell functions or subshells, allowing errors inside functions to bypass the error handler. Why this gets asked: rarely known; demonstrates hands-on debugging experience."],["14.html#strict-mode","How do you find the line number where a script failed?","Questions · Pro","How do you find the line number where a script failed? trap 'echo \"line $LINENO\"' ERR combined with set -E. Bash does not provide built-in stack traces. Why this gets asked: evaluates how you triage production failures."],["14.html#strict-mode","How do you obtain the exit code of a specific stage in a pipeline?","Questions · Pro","How do you obtain the exit code of a specific stage in a pipeline? From the ${PIPESTATUS[@]} array immediately following the pipeline - any subsequent command overwrites it. Why this gets asked: classic differentiator; separates those who have read man bash thoroughly."],["14.html#strict-mode","Why does a script with set -e silently exit on ((i++))?","Questions · Pro","Why does a script with set -e silently exit on ((i++))? Arithmetic evaluation returns exit code 1 if the arithmetic result is 0, and post-increment i++ on i=0 yields 0. The safe form is i=$((i + 1)): variable assignments always return exit code 0. Why this gets asked: classic trap: the script crashes without a single error message on a loop counter."],["14.html#strict-mode","What does trap … EXIT do and why is it more reliable than manual cleanup?","Questions · Pro","What does trap … EXIT do and why is it more reliable than manual cleanup? It executes upon any termination the shell can detect: normal completion, error exits, or signals. The exception is SIGKILL: under kill -9, the OOM killer, or abrupt container termination, the trap does not run and temporary files remain. Why this gets asked: checks whether you reason through all exit paths, including unpreventable termination."],["14.html#strict-mode","How does exit differ from return inside a function?","Questions · Pro","How does exit differ from return inside a function? return terminates the function; exit terminates the entire process. However, a function invoked via command substitution $() runs in a subshell, so its exit only terminates the subshell, leaving the parent script running. Why this gets asked: tests understanding of process isolation on the most common edge case."],["14.html#strict-mode","What does exit code 130 signify?","Questions · Pro","What does exit code 130 signify? Termination by signal: 128 + N where N is the signal number; 130 corresponds to SIGINT (e.g. Ctrl+C). Why this gets asked: demonstrates ability to read exit codes in CI logs."],["14.html#strict-mode","What is a zombie process and why does kill -9 fail to remove it?","Questions · Pro","What is a zombie process and why does kill -9 fail to remove it? It is an already-terminated process whose exit status has not yet been collected by its parent via wait. There is nothing to kill - it is dead; it disappears once the parent waits on it or the parent terminates. In containers lacking an init system, nobody collects orphans, causing zombies on PID 1. Why this gets asked: connects signals, wait, and container PID 1 responsibilities in a single question."],["14.html#expansions-arrays","Expansions, arrays, and quoting (11–20)","Questions · Pro","Expansions, arrays, and quoting (11–20) How does \"$@\" differ from $@ and \"$*\"? \"$@\" expands to separate positional arguments preserving word boundaries; $@ splits on IFS; \"$*\" collapses all arguments into a single string separated by the first character of IFS. Why this gets asked: one of the most frequent interview questions; the answer instantly reveals skill level. Why do professional scripts prefer printf over echo? Behavior of echo with flags like -n, -e, or trailing backslashes diverges across shells and operating systems; printf is strictly defined by POSIX and behaves consistently. Why this gets asked: universal portability question asked in almost every assessment. Why include IFS=$'\\n\\t' at the start of a script? It restricts field splitting to newlines and tabs, eliminating whitespace splitting where it is almost never desired. Why this gets asked: a staple of canonical strict mode configurations. Why does arr=($(cmd)) fail and what should replace it? Command substitution output undergoes word splitting and glob expansion: whitespace-separated items split into multiple elements, and * expands to filenames. Replace with mapfile -t arr < <(cmd): one array element per line, with no globbing. Why this gets asked: distinguishes knowledge of array syntax from understanding why dirty data corrupts arrays. Why does df show a full disk while du shows ample free space? Two common causes: an unlinked file still held open by an active process prevents disk block deallocation while remaining invisible to du (locate via lsof +L1); and exhausted inode tables despite free bytes (inspect via df -i). Why this gets asked: foundational question during incident triage interviews. What is an associative array and in which Bash version was it introduced? declare -A m; m[key]=val implements key-value dictionaries; introduced in Bash 4.0. Why this gets asked: critical for macOS compatibility, which ships with Bash 3.2 by default. What does ${var:?message} do? Aborts script execution with an error printing message if the variable is unset or null. Why this gets asked: concise idiomatic assertion for mandatory script parameters. How does ${var%.txt} differ from ${var#*/}? The former strips matching suffix patterns; the latter strips matching prefix patterns; doubling the symbol enables greedy matching. Why this gets asked: pure builtin string manipulation replacing external subshell calls like basename. How do you validate a string against a regular expression without grep? [[ $s =~ ^v([0-9]+) ]]; captured groups are stored in ${BASH_REMATCH[@]}. The right-hand regex must not be quoted - quoting forces literal string comparison. Why this gets asked: tests knowledge of built-in pattern matching and a subtle quoting trap. Why is eval considered hazardous? It executes a string as code after performing all expansions, converting untrusted input into executed commands. Safer alternatives exist almost always: arrays for argument lists, printf -v, or namerefs ( declare -n). Why this gets asked: checks whether you can spot remote code injection vulnerabilities."],["14.html#expansions-arrays","How does \"$@\" differ from $@ and \"$*\"?","Questions · Pro","How does \"$@\" differ from $@ and \"$*\"? \"$@\" expands to separate positional arguments preserving word boundaries; $@ splits on IFS; \"$*\" collapses all arguments into a single string separated by the first character of IFS. Why this gets asked: one of the most frequent interview questions; the answer instantly reveals skill level."],["14.html#expansions-arrays","Why do professional scripts prefer printf over echo?","Questions · Pro","Why do professional scripts prefer printf over echo? Behavior of echo with flags like -n, -e, or trailing backslashes diverges across shells and operating systems; printf is strictly defined by POSIX and behaves consistently. Why this gets asked: universal portability question asked in almost every assessment."],["14.html#expansions-arrays","Why include IFS=$'\\n\\t' at the start of a script?","Questions · Pro","Why include IFS=$'\\n\\t' at the start of a script? It restricts field splitting to newlines and tabs, eliminating whitespace splitting where it is almost never desired. Why this gets asked: a staple of canonical strict mode configurations."],["14.html#expansions-arrays","Why does arr=($(cmd)) fail and what should replace it?","Questions · Pro","Why does arr=($(cmd)) fail and what should replace it? Command substitution output undergoes word splitting and glob expansion: whitespace-separated items split into multiple elements, and * expands to filenames. Replace with mapfile -t arr < <(cmd): one array element per line, with no globbing. Why this gets asked: distinguishes knowledge of array syntax from understanding why dirty data corrupts arrays."],["14.html#expansions-arrays","Why does df show a full disk while du shows ample free space?","Questions · Pro","Why does df show a full disk while du shows ample free space? Two common causes: an unlinked file still held open by an active process prevents disk block deallocation while remaining invisible to du (locate via lsof +L1); and exhausted inode tables despite free bytes (inspect via df -i). Why this gets asked: foundational question during incident triage interviews."],["14.html#expansions-arrays","What is an associative array and in which Bash version was it introduced?","Questions · Pro","What is an associative array and in which Bash version was it introduced? declare -A m; m[key]=val implements key-value dictionaries; introduced in Bash 4.0. Why this gets asked: critical for macOS compatibility, which ships with Bash 3.2 by default."],["14.html#expansions-arrays","What does ${var:?message} do?","Questions · Pro","What does ${var:?message} do? Aborts script execution with an error printing message if the variable is unset or null. Why this gets asked: concise idiomatic assertion for mandatory script parameters."],["14.html#expansions-arrays","How does ${var%.txt} differ from ${var#*/}?","Questions · Pro","How does ${var%.txt} differ from ${var#*/}? The former strips matching suffix patterns; the latter strips matching prefix patterns; doubling the symbol enables greedy matching. Why this gets asked: pure builtin string manipulation replacing external subshell calls like basename."],["14.html#expansions-arrays","How do you validate a string against a regular expression without grep?","Questions · Pro","How do you validate a string against a regular expression without grep? [[ $s =~ ^v([0-9]+) ]]; captured groups are stored in ${BASH_REMATCH[@]}. The right-hand regex must not be quoted - quoting forces literal string comparison. Why this gets asked: tests knowledge of built-in pattern matching and a subtle quoting trap."],["14.html#expansions-arrays","Why is eval considered hazardous?","Questions · Pro","Why is eval considered hazardous? It executes a string as code after performing all expansions, converting untrusted input into executed commands. Safer alternatives exist almost always: arrays for argument lists, printf -v, or namerefs ( declare -n). Why this gets asked: checks whether you can spot remote code injection vulnerabilities."],["14.html#processes-pipelines","Processes, pipelines, and redirections (21–30)","Questions · Pro","Processes, pipelines, and redirections (21–30) In what order does Bash execute expansions? Five distinct stages: brace expansion; then a single left-to-right pass performing tilde expansion, parameter expansion, command substitution, arithmetic expansion, and process substitution; followed by word splitting; pathname expansion; and quote removal. That parameter and arithmetic expansion occur in the same pass is proven by x=1; echo $((x=2)) $x, which outputs 2 2. Why this gets asked: explains nearly all shell quirks at once; claiming “eight sequential steps” is a common inaccuracy. Why does {1..$n} fail to expand? Brace expansion executes before parameter expansion, so $n has not yet been replaced with its value. Why this gets asked: tests understanding of expansion precedence rather than mere syntax memorization. How does cmd >log 2>&1 differ from cmd 2>&1 >log? In the former, both descriptors redirect to the file; in the latter, stderr duplicates stdout's initial target (the terminal), and only stdout redirects to the file. Why this gets asked: classic test of file descriptor duplication mechanics. What is a here-document and what is its primary pitfall? <<EOF feeds multiline text to stdin; <<< feeds a single string. The pitfall is delimiter quoting: with 'EOF' text is literal; without quotes, $var and $(cmd) expand, silently corrupting embedded dollar signs in SQL or configs. Why this gets asked: checks whether you know when content mutates during ingestion. Why is a variable modified inside a loop after | empty outside the loop? Pipeline stages execute inside subshells; environment mutations do not propagate back to the parent process. Why this gets asked: one of the most frequently discussed Bash architectural puzzles. How do you stream a command's output into a loop without spawning a subshell? Using process substitution: while read -r l; do …; done < <(cmd). Why this gets asked: tests mastery of process substitution <() - a hallmark of seniority. How does exec without arguments differ from exec cmd? Without a command, it reconfigures file descriptors for the current shell; with a command, it replaces the current process image without spawning a new PID. Why this gets asked: critical for container entrypoint wrappers. How do you enforce a execution time ceiling on a command? timeout 30 cmd; on expiration, it exits with status code 124. Why this gets asked: mandatory safeguard for network and external service operations. How do you execute a process so it survives terminal session termination? nohup cmd &, setsid, or a terminal multiplexer; in systemd, launch it as a dedicated service unit. Why this gets asked: tests understanding of controlling terminals and SIGHUP signals. What does wait -n do? It pauses until any single background job completes rather than waiting for all jobs (Bash 4.3+). Why this gets asked: necessary for implementing custom worker pool limits."],["14.html#processes-pipelines","In what order does Bash execute expansions?","Questions · Pro","In what order does Bash execute expansions? Five distinct stages: brace expansion; then a single left-to-right pass performing tilde expansion, parameter expansion, command substitution, arithmetic expansion, and process substitution; followed by word splitting; pathname expansion; and quote removal. That parameter and arithmetic expansion occur in the same pass is proven by x=1; echo $((x=2)) $x, which outputs 2 2. Why this gets asked: explains nearly all shell quirks at once; claiming “eight sequential steps” is a common inaccuracy."],["14.html#processes-pipelines","Why does {1..$n} fail to expand?","Questions · Pro","Why does {1..$n} fail to expand? Brace expansion executes before parameter expansion, so $n has not yet been replaced with its value. Why this gets asked: tests understanding of expansion precedence rather than mere syntax memorization."],["14.html#processes-pipelines","How does cmd >log 2>&1 differ from cmd 2>&1 >log?","Questions · Pro","How does cmd >log 2>&1 differ from cmd 2>&1 >log? In the former, both descriptors redirect to the file; in the latter, stderr duplicates stdout's initial target (the terminal), and only stdout redirects to the file. Why this gets asked: classic test of file descriptor duplication mechanics."],["14.html#processes-pipelines","What is a here-document and what is its primary pitfall?","Questions · Pro","What is a here-document and what is its primary pitfall? <<EOF feeds multiline text to stdin; <<< feeds a single string. The pitfall is delimiter quoting: with 'EOF' text is literal; without quotes, $var and $(cmd) expand, silently corrupting embedded dollar signs in SQL or configs. Why this gets asked: checks whether you know when content mutates during ingestion."],["14.html#processes-pipelines","Why is a variable modified inside a loop after | empty outside the loop?","Questions · Pro","Why is a variable modified inside a loop after | empty outside the loop? Pipeline stages execute inside subshells; environment mutations do not propagate back to the parent process. Why this gets asked: one of the most frequently discussed Bash architectural puzzles."],["14.html#processes-pipelines","How do you stream a command's output into a loop without spawning a subshell?","Questions · Pro","How do you stream a command's output into a loop without spawning a subshell? Using process substitution: while read -r l; do …; done < <(cmd). Why this gets asked: tests mastery of process substitution <() - a hallmark of seniority."],["14.html#processes-pipelines","How does exec without arguments differ from exec cmd?","Questions · Pro","How does exec without arguments differ from exec cmd? Without a command, it reconfigures file descriptors for the current shell; with a command, it replaces the current process image without spawning a new PID. Why this gets asked: critical for container entrypoint wrappers."],["14.html#processes-pipelines","How do you enforce a execution time ceiling on a command?","Questions · Pro","How do you enforce a execution time ceiling on a command? timeout 30 cmd; on expiration, it exits with status code 124. Why this gets asked: mandatory safeguard for network and external service operations."],["14.html#processes-pipelines","How do you execute a process so it survives terminal session termination?","Questions · Pro","How do you execute a process so it survives terminal session termination? nohup cmd &, setsid, or a terminal multiplexer; in systemd, launch it as a dedicated service unit. Why this gets asked: tests understanding of controlling terminals and SIGHUP signals."],["14.html#processes-pipelines","What does wait -n do?","Questions · Pro","What does wait -n do? It pauses until any single background job completes rather than waiting for all jobs (Bash 4.3+). Why this gets asked: necessary for implementing custom worker pool limits."],["14.html#files-locking","Files, concurrency, and locking (31–40)","Questions · Pro","Files, concurrency, and locking (31–40) Why should temporary files always be generated via mktemp? It guarantees cryptographically random filenames and restrictive file permissions; predictable paths in /tmp introduce race conditions and symlink hijacking exploits. Why this gets asked: tests awareness of both operational reliability and system security. How do you prevent concurrent execution of multiple script instances? Bind flock to a file descriptor: the lock releases automatically upon process termination, unlike brittle flag files. Why this gets asked: essential requirement for cron jobs and schedulers. How does mv differ from cp regarding atomicity? Renaming within the same filesystem is atomic; copying is not, exposing partially written intermediate files to concurrent readers. Why this gets asked: foundational pattern for zero-downtime deployment cutovers and config generation. How do you atomically switch a symlink to point to a new release? ln -sfn new tmp && mv -T tmp current - updates the symlink target in a single atomic filesystem operation. Why this gets asked: checks whether you consider intermediate system states during deployments. How do you safely process filenames containing spaces and newlines? Use null delimiters: find … -print0 piped to xargs -0 or parsed with read -d ''. Why this gets asked: indicates real-world experience handling unhygienic filesystem data. Why is xargs -P superior to looping with background &? It throttles maximum concurrent workers and returns a non-zero exit code if any worker job fails. Why this gets asked: standard production technique for managed parallelism. How do you verify whether a background process failed? Capture its PID via $! and invoke wait $PID - returning the exit status. In contrast, argumentless wait always returns 0, even if background jobs failed. Why this gets asked: verifies understanding that bare wait silently swallows failures. How does locale affect sort, grep, and ranges like [a-z]? Collation order and range definitions vary across locales, causing identical scripts to produce diverging results across hosts. Enforce predictability by setting LC_ALL=C. Why this gets asked: notorious source of “works on my machine” discrepancies that are difficult to isolate without deep knowledge. How does find -exec cmd {} + differ from -exec cmd {} \\;? \\; spawns a separate process per matched file; + aggregates files into batches similar to xargs. Across thousands of files, + executes orders of magnitude faster while handling spaces safely. Why this gets asked: evaluates understanding of process spawning overhead. What happens to for f in *.log if no matching files exist, and what do nullglob and failglob change? By default, the pattern remains an unexpanded literal string *.log, causing the loop to execute once on a non-existent path. shopt -s nullglob expands unmatched globs to an empty list; failglob raises an error. Why this gets asked: classic trap where scripts erroneously process missing files."],["14.html#files-locking","Why should temporary files always be generated via mktemp?","Questions · Pro","Why should temporary files always be generated via mktemp? It guarantees cryptographically random filenames and restrictive file permissions; predictable paths in /tmp introduce race conditions and symlink hijacking exploits. Why this gets asked: tests awareness of both operational reliability and system security."],["14.html#files-locking","How do you prevent concurrent execution of multiple script instances?","Questions · Pro","How do you prevent concurrent execution of multiple script instances? Bind flock to a file descriptor: the lock releases automatically upon process termination, unlike brittle flag files. Why this gets asked: essential requirement for cron jobs and schedulers."],["14.html#files-locking","How does mv differ from cp regarding atomicity?","Questions · Pro","How does mv differ from cp regarding atomicity? Renaming within the same filesystem is atomic; copying is not, exposing partially written intermediate files to concurrent readers. Why this gets asked: foundational pattern for zero-downtime deployment cutovers and config generation."],["14.html#files-locking","How do you atomically switch a symlink to point to a new release?","Questions · Pro","How do you atomically switch a symlink to point to a new release? ln -sfn new tmp && mv -T tmp current - updates the symlink target in a single atomic filesystem operation. Why this gets asked: checks whether you consider intermediate system states during deployments."],["14.html#files-locking","How do you safely process filenames containing spaces and newlines?","Questions · Pro","How do you safely process filenames containing spaces and newlines? Use null delimiters: find … -print0 piped to xargs -0 or parsed with read -d ''. Why this gets asked: indicates real-world experience handling unhygienic filesystem data."],["14.html#files-locking","Why is xargs -P superior to looping with background &?","Questions · Pro","Why is xargs -P superior to looping with background &? It throttles maximum concurrent workers and returns a non-zero exit code if any worker job fails. Why this gets asked: standard production technique for managed parallelism."],["14.html#files-locking","How do you verify whether a background process failed?","Questions · Pro","How do you verify whether a background process failed? Capture its PID via $! and invoke wait $PID - returning the exit status. In contrast, argumentless wait always returns 0, even if background jobs failed. Why this gets asked: verifies understanding that bare wait silently swallows failures."],["14.html#files-locking","How does locale affect sort, grep, and ranges like [a-z]?","Questions · Pro","How does locale affect sort, grep, and ranges like [a-z]? Collation order and range definitions vary across locales, causing identical scripts to produce diverging results across hosts. Enforce predictability by setting LC_ALL=C. Why this gets asked: notorious source of “works on my machine” discrepancies that are difficult to isolate without deep knowledge."],["14.html#files-locking","How does find -exec cmd {} + differ from -exec cmd {} \\;?","Questions · Pro","How does find -exec cmd {} + differ from -exec cmd {} \\;? \\; spawns a separate process per matched file; + aggregates files into batches similar to xargs. Across thousands of files, + executes orders of magnitude faster while handling spaces safely. Why this gets asked: evaluates understanding of process spawning overhead."],["14.html#files-locking","What happens to for f in *.log if no matching files exist, and what do nullglob and failglob change?","Questions · Pro","What happens to for f in *.log if no matching files exist, and what do nullglob and failglob change? By default, the pattern remains an unexpanded literal string *.log, causing the loop to execute once on a non-existent path. shopt -s nullglob expands unmatched globs to an empty list; failglob raises an error. Why this gets asked: classic trap where scripts erroneously process missing files."],["14.html#ci-containers","CI, containers, systemd, and secrets (41–50)","Questions · Pro","CI, containers, systemd, and secrets (41–50) With what flags does CI execute a run: step? In GitHub Actions without an explicit shell:, it executes bash -e {0}; with shell: bash, it runs bash --noprofile --norc -eo pipefail {0}. The -u flag is never enabled automatically. Why this gets asked: checks whether you inspect runner documentation rather than assuming shell flags. Why is an export in one CI step invisible in subsequent steps? Each step executes in an isolated process; persist environment variables across steps by writing to runner files like $GITHUB_ENV. Why this gets asked: textbook mistake when transitioning from local development to CI automation. Why does a container fail to shut down gracefully upon docker stop? When using the shell form of ENTRYPOINT, PID 1 is the shell process, which fails to forward SIGTERM to application children. Use the exec array form or prepend exec in entrypoint scripts. Why this gets asked: favorite interview topic in container and Kubernetes assessments. Why do scripts fail when running in Alpine Linux images? Alpine uses busybox ash as /bin/sh; Bash is not installed by default, and core utility options diverge from GNU coreutils. Why this gets asked: evaluates awareness of distribution differences in minimal container images. Why do redirections fail inside systemd ExecStart directives? Directives are parsed directly by systemd rather than passed through a shell; wrap commands explicitly with /bin/bash -c '…'. Why this gets asked: frequent pitfall when authoring systemd service units. How do you securely supply secrets to a command? Pass them via stdin, credentials files, or environment variables - never as command arguments, which are visible in ps to all local users. Why this gets asked: non-negotiable security baseline evaluated in every engineering interview. Why is set -x dangerous in CI environments? Execution tracing prints expanded variables, exposing secrets directly in CI build logs, while CI masking only redacts exact string matches. Why this gets asked: tests whether you treat build logs as public audit surfaces. How does /bin/sh differ from /bin/bash on Debian/Ubuntu systems? sh symlinks to dash: lacking arrays, [[ ]], process substitution, and ${v//a/b} pattern replacement. Contrary to popular misconception, local is supported in dash. Why this gets asked: core portability knowledge; even seasoned engineers trip on local support in dash. How do you validate a shell script without executing it? bash -n script.sh checks syntax; shellcheck performs deep static analysis catching semantic bugs and pitfalls. Why this gets asked: checks for ingrained static analysis and linting habits. What is # shellcheck disable=SCxxxx and how should it be applied? It suppresses a specific rule for the immediate next line; applying global file-level suppressions almost always masks legitimate defects. Why this gets asked: demonstrates your engineering posture toward automated quality gates."],["14.html#ci-containers","With what flags does CI execute a run: step?","Questions · Pro","With what flags does CI execute a run: step? In GitHub Actions without an explicit shell:, it executes bash -e {0}; with shell: bash, it runs bash --noprofile --norc -eo pipefail {0}. The -u flag is never enabled automatically. Why this gets asked: checks whether you inspect runner documentation rather than assuming shell flags."],["14.html#ci-containers","Why is an export in one CI step invisible in subsequent steps?","Questions · Pro","Why is an export in one CI step invisible in subsequent steps? Each step executes in an isolated process; persist environment variables across steps by writing to runner files like $GITHUB_ENV. Why this gets asked: textbook mistake when transitioning from local development to CI automation."],["14.html#ci-containers","Why does a container fail to shut down gracefully upon docker stop?","Questions · Pro","Why does a container fail to shut down gracefully upon docker stop? When using the shell form of ENTRYPOINT, PID 1 is the shell process, which fails to forward SIGTERM to application children. Use the exec array form or prepend exec in entrypoint scripts. Why this gets asked: favorite interview topic in container and Kubernetes assessments."],["14.html#ci-containers","Why do scripts fail when running in Alpine Linux images?","Questions · Pro","Why do scripts fail when running in Alpine Linux images? Alpine uses busybox ash as /bin/sh; Bash is not installed by default, and core utility options diverge from GNU coreutils. Why this gets asked: evaluates awareness of distribution differences in minimal container images."],["14.html#ci-containers","Why do redirections fail inside systemd ExecStart directives?","Questions · Pro","Why do redirections fail inside systemd ExecStart directives? Directives are parsed directly by systemd rather than passed through a shell; wrap commands explicitly with /bin/bash -c '…'. Why this gets asked: frequent pitfall when authoring systemd service units."],["14.html#ci-containers","How do you securely supply secrets to a command?","Questions · Pro","How do you securely supply secrets to a command? Pass them via stdin, credentials files, or environment variables - never as command arguments, which are visible in ps to all local users. Why this gets asked: non-negotiable security baseline evaluated in every engineering interview."],["14.html#ci-containers","Why is set -x dangerous in CI environments?","Questions · Pro","Why is set -x dangerous in CI environments? Execution tracing prints expanded variables, exposing secrets directly in CI build logs, while CI masking only redacts exact string matches. Why this gets asked: tests whether you treat build logs as public audit surfaces."],["14.html#ci-containers","How does /bin/sh differ from /bin/bash on Debian/Ubuntu systems?","Questions · Pro","How does /bin/sh differ from /bin/bash on Debian/Ubuntu systems? sh symlinks to dash: lacking arrays, [[ ]], process substitution, and ${v//a/b} pattern replacement. Contrary to popular misconception, local is supported in dash. Why this gets asked: core portability knowledge; even seasoned engineers trip on local support in dash."],["14.html#ci-containers","How do you validate a shell script without executing it?","Questions · Pro","How do you validate a shell script without executing it? bash -n script.sh checks syntax; shellcheck performs deep static analysis catching semantic bugs and pitfalls. Why this gets asked: checks for ingrained static analysis and linting habits."],["14.html#ci-containers","What is # shellcheck disable=SCxxxx and how should it be applied?","Questions · Pro","What is # shellcheck disable=SCxxxx and how should it be applied? It suppresses a specific rule for the immediate next line; applying global file-level suppressions almost always masks legitimate defects. Why this gets asked: demonstrates your engineering posture toward automated quality gates."],["14.html#artifacts","Artifacts: what you take away from this page","Questions · Pro","Artifacts: what you take away from this page Reading >> Your life quantum · Dignity partnership with AI · Rewiring your neural network Fifty failure scenarios and the concrete answer to each. Your time was well spent if you stopped answering “that never happens” and started naming the exact preconditions under which it does. That is the essence of rewiring: replacing “what does this command do” with “what will happen when this goes wrong.” Ten answers on strict mode - including holes in set -e and the -E flag. Ten on expansions and arrays - \"$@\", passing arrays, ${var@Q}. Ten on processes and descriptors - expansion order, 2>&1, process substitution. Ten on files and concurrency - mktemp, flock, atomicity, -print0. Ten on runtime environments - CI, container PID 1, systemd, secrets, dash versus bash."],["15.html#applicability","Applicability decisions (1–10)","Questions · IGL","Applicability decisions (1–10) By what criteria do you decide that a task is not suited for Bash? Data structures beyond flat lists, floating-point arithmetic, structured error recovery, concurrency requiring inter-task communication, or logic exceeding three hundred lines. Why this gets asked: tests for concrete technical criteria rather than personal taste. When is a script the right choice even though a mature tool already exists? When the task is strictly one-off, when deploying the tool introduces prohibitive bootstrapping friction, or when running in a bare environment where nothing else is installed yet. Why this gets asked: checks whether you swing to the opposite dogmatic extreme. How does an action-oriented problem differ from a state-oriented problem? An action executes once and finishes; state must be continuously maintained, reconciled against desired state, and tracked. Managing state is not a problem for a script. Why this gets asked: fundamental boundary separating Bash from Terraform and configuration management. How do you respond to a request to “write a quick script that monitors and fixes things”? Point out that this describes a state control reconciliation loop, which already has mature solutions; a script here will simply devolve into a poorly implemented Terraform or Kubernetes controller. Why this gets asked: evaluates your ability to reframe problems rather than blindly implementing them. How do you assess the total cost of ownership of a shell script? By the number of engineers who will have to read and maintain it, the frequency of required modifications, and the blast radius of failure - not by line count. Why this gets asked: reflects economic and lifecycle engineering thinking. When is rewriting a functioning script an engineering mistake? When it is small, stable, and well-understood: maintenance overhead scales with complexity and churn, not programming language. Why this gets asked: tests whether you succumb to rewriting out of dogma or aesthetic bias. How do you choose between Make and a sequential shell script? Choose Make when dealing with dependency directed acyclic graphs and incremental execution; choose a script when execution steps are strictly linear. Why this gets asked: common antipattern is adopting Makefiles purely as command task runners without dependency semantics. When should logic be extracted from CI workflow YAML into repository scripts? As soon as it cannot be reproduced and debugged locally using the exact same command: this is an absolute requirement for reproducibility. Why this gets asked: evaluates understanding of the boundary between pipeline orchestration and application code. What do you do when identical shell logic is copy-pasted across three CI pipelines? Extract it into a shared, versioned script, container image, or reusable CI action; duplicating logic guarantees three diverging implementations within six months. Why this gets asked: tests discipline against code duplication at the infrastructure tier. How do you decide between authoring an in-house tool versus adopting an existing solution? By comparing ongoing maintenance costs and the organizational cost of the author departing the team, rather than initial implementation hours. Why this gets asked: tests long-term architectural planning horizons."],["15.html#applicability","By what criteria do you decide that a task is not suited for Bash?","Questions · IGL","By what criteria do you decide that a task is not suited for Bash? Data structures beyond flat lists, floating-point arithmetic, structured error recovery, concurrency requiring inter-task communication, or logic exceeding three hundred lines. Why this gets asked: tests for concrete technical criteria rather than personal taste."],["15.html#applicability","When is a script the right choice even though a mature tool already exists?","Questions · IGL","When is a script the right choice even though a mature tool already exists? When the task is strictly one-off, when deploying the tool introduces prohibitive bootstrapping friction, or when running in a bare environment where nothing else is installed yet. Why this gets asked: checks whether you swing to the opposite dogmatic extreme."],["15.html#applicability","How does an action-oriented problem differ from a state-oriented problem?","Questions · IGL","How does an action-oriented problem differ from a state-oriented problem? An action executes once and finishes; state must be continuously maintained, reconciled against desired state, and tracked. Managing state is not a problem for a script. Why this gets asked: fundamental boundary separating Bash from Terraform and configuration management."],["15.html#applicability","How do you respond to a request to “write a quick script that monitors and fixes things”?","Questions · IGL","How do you respond to a request to “write a quick script that monitors and fixes things”? Point out that this describes a state control reconciliation loop, which already has mature solutions; a script here will simply devolve into a poorly implemented Terraform or Kubernetes controller. Why this gets asked: evaluates your ability to reframe problems rather than blindly implementing them."],["15.html#applicability","How do you assess the total cost of ownership of a shell script?","Questions · IGL","How do you assess the total cost of ownership of a shell script? By the number of engineers who will have to read and maintain it, the frequency of required modifications, and the blast radius of failure - not by line count. Why this gets asked: reflects economic and lifecycle engineering thinking."],["15.html#applicability","When is rewriting a functioning script an engineering mistake?","Questions · IGL","When is rewriting a functioning script an engineering mistake? When it is small, stable, and well-understood: maintenance overhead scales with complexity and churn, not programming language. Why this gets asked: tests whether you succumb to rewriting out of dogma or aesthetic bias."],["15.html#applicability","How do you choose between Make and a sequential shell script?","Questions · IGL","How do you choose between Make and a sequential shell script? Choose Make when dealing with dependency directed acyclic graphs and incremental execution; choose a script when execution steps are strictly linear. Why this gets asked: common antipattern is adopting Makefiles purely as command task runners without dependency semantics."],["15.html#applicability","When should logic be extracted from CI workflow YAML into repository scripts?","Questions · IGL","When should logic be extracted from CI workflow YAML into repository scripts? As soon as it cannot be reproduced and debugged locally using the exact same command: this is an absolute requirement for reproducibility. Why this gets asked: evaluates understanding of the boundary between pipeline orchestration and application code."],["15.html#applicability","What do you do when identical shell logic is copy-pasted across three CI pipelines?","Questions · IGL","What do you do when identical shell logic is copy-pasted across three CI pipelines? Extract it into a shared, versioned script, container image, or reusable CI action; duplicating logic guarantees three diverging implementations within six months. Why this gets asked: tests discipline against code duplication at the infrastructure tier."],["15.html#applicability","How do you decide between authoring an in-house tool versus adopting an existing solution?","Questions · IGL","How do you decide between authoring an in-house tool versus adopting an existing solution? By comparing ongoing maintenance costs and the organizational cost of the author departing the team, rather than initial implementation hours. Why this gets asked: tests long-term architectural planning horizons."],["15.html#contract-compat","Contract and compatibility (11–20)","Questions · IGL","Contract and compatibility (11–20) What constitutes the contract of a script intended for machine invocation? Standard stream allocation (stdout for data, stderr for diagnostics), explicit exit code semantics, structured machine-readable output formats, a dry-run flag, and documented backward compatibility guarantees. Why this gets asked: tests whether you treat scripts as formal software interfaces. How do you distinguish “not found” from “broken execution” in exit codes? Use distinct exit codes: a predictable negative result returns one code (e.g., 1), while an internal failure returns another (e.g., 2); callers must handle these cases differently. Why this gets asked: demonstrates designing for the upstream caller rather than immediate convenience. Why should a script provide a --json flag if human output can already be parsed with awk/grep? So human-facing diagnostic text can evolve freely without breaking automation; without this separation, every log tweak becomes a breaking change. Why this gets asked: foundational API and interface stability principle. What constitutes a breaking change in an automation script? Changing exit code meanings, altering stdout formatting, renaming flags or environment variables, and modifying default behavior - anything downstream callers depend upon. Why this gets asked: evaluates understanding of backward compatibility contracts. How do you introduce new behavior without breaking existing consumers? Introduce a new opt-in flag with a safe default, issue deprecation warnings to stderr announcing future default changes with a target date, and only switch defaults in a subsequent major release. Why this gets asked: demonstrates experience with non-disruptive interface evolution. Why should a utility script support --version? To allow callers to verify compatibility requirements and ensure that incident issue reports are unambiguously reproducible. Implementing --version costs trivial effort. Why this gets asked: a small polish detail that distinguishes a maintained tool from a throwaway file. Why should --dry-run be designed into the architecture from the start rather than retrofitted? Retrofitted dry-run modes frequently miss dangerous conditional branches; wrapping every mutating command structurally ensures comprehensive coverage. However, wrappers do not catch redirections: run cmd > file truncates the file even in dry-run mode because the shell performs redirection before the wrapper executes. File writes must be moved inside the invoked command. Why this gets asked: checks whether testability is an upfront architectural concern - and whether you know shell redirection semantics. How do you document a script intended to serve as a shared engineering tool? A built-in --help flag and concise header comments; external wiki pages and tickets rot far faster than code. Why this gets asked: pragmatic utility outweighs exhaustive documentation. How do you guarantee identical behavior locally and in CI runners? Run the exact same command inside the exact same container image environment; diverging dependencies erode trust in test suites. Why this gets asked: addresses the primary root cause of “works on my machine” syndrome. Why does a shared common.sh library sourced across a dozen repositories become an architectural trap? Bash lacks namespaces and package versioning: source pollutes caller scopes with global variables and unintended set options, while modifying the shared library instantly destabilizes every consumer. Shared logic should be distributed as versioned container images, packaged CLIs, or CI actions. Why this gets asked: architectural question regarding shared code in languages without package managers."],["15.html#contract-compat","What constitutes the contract of a script intended for machine invocation?","Questions · IGL","What constitutes the contract of a script intended for machine invocation? Standard stream allocation (stdout for data, stderr for diagnostics), explicit exit code semantics, structured machine-readable output formats, a dry-run flag, and documented backward compatibility guarantees. Why this gets asked: tests whether you treat scripts as formal software interfaces."],["15.html#contract-compat","How do you distinguish “not found” from “broken execution” in exit codes?","Questions · IGL","How do you distinguish “not found” from “broken execution” in exit codes? Use distinct exit codes: a predictable negative result returns one code (e.g., 1), while an internal failure returns another (e.g., 2); callers must handle these cases differently. Why this gets asked: demonstrates designing for the upstream caller rather than immediate convenience."],["15.html#contract-compat","Why should a script provide a --json flag if human output can already be parsed with awk/grep?","Questions · IGL","Why should a script provide a --json flag if human output can already be parsed with awk/grep? So human-facing diagnostic text can evolve freely without breaking automation; without this separation, every log tweak becomes a breaking change. Why this gets asked: foundational API and interface stability principle."],["15.html#contract-compat","What constitutes a breaking change in an automation script?","Questions · IGL","What constitutes a breaking change in an automation script? Changing exit code meanings, altering stdout formatting, renaming flags or environment variables, and modifying default behavior - anything downstream callers depend upon. Why this gets asked: evaluates understanding of backward compatibility contracts."],["15.html#contract-compat","How do you introduce new behavior without breaking existing consumers?","Questions · IGL","How do you introduce new behavior without breaking existing consumers? Introduce a new opt-in flag with a safe default, issue deprecation warnings to stderr announcing future default changes with a target date, and only switch defaults in a subsequent major release. Why this gets asked: demonstrates experience with non-disruptive interface evolution."],["15.html#contract-compat","Why should a utility script support --version?","Questions · IGL","Why should a utility script support --version? To allow callers to verify compatibility requirements and ensure that incident issue reports are unambiguously reproducible. Implementing --version costs trivial effort. Why this gets asked: a small polish detail that distinguishes a maintained tool from a throwaway file."],["15.html#contract-compat","Why should --dry-run be designed into the architecture from the start rather than retrofitted?","Questions · IGL","Why should --dry-run be designed into the architecture from the start rather than retrofitted? Retrofitted dry-run modes frequently miss dangerous conditional branches; wrapping every mutating command structurally ensures comprehensive coverage. However, wrappers do not catch redirections: run cmd > file truncates the file even in dry-run mode because the shell performs redirection before the wrapper executes. File writes must be moved inside the invoked command. Why this gets asked: checks whether testability is an upfront architectural concern - and whether you know shell redirection semantics."],["15.html#contract-compat","How do you document a script intended to serve as a shared engineering tool?","Questions · IGL","How do you document a script intended to serve as a shared engineering tool? A built-in --help flag and concise header comments; external wiki pages and tickets rot far faster than code. Why this gets asked: pragmatic utility outweighs exhaustive documentation."],["15.html#contract-compat","How do you guarantee identical behavior locally and in CI runners?","Questions · IGL","How do you guarantee identical behavior locally and in CI runners? Run the exact same command inside the exact same container image environment; diverging dependencies erode trust in test suites. Why this gets asked: addresses the primary root cause of “works on my machine” syndrome."],["15.html#contract-compat","Why does a shared common.sh library sourced across a dozen repositories become an architectural trap?","Questions · IGL","Why does a shared common.sh library sourced across a dozen repositories become an architectural trap? Bash lacks namespaces and package versioning: source pollutes caller scopes with global variables and unintended set options, while modifying the shared library instantly destabilizes every consumer. Shared logic should be distributed as versioned container images, packaged CLIs, or CI actions. Why this gets asked: architectural question regarding shared code in languages without package managers."],["15.html#reliability","Reliability and idempotence (21–30)","Questions · IGL","Reliability and idempotence (21–30) What is idempotence in the context of shell scripting? Executing the script repeatedly yields the same system state without introducing errors, since retries are inevitable in distributed systems. Why this gets asked: central property of robust infrastructure automation. How do you make a non-idempotent operation safe for retries? Introduce an idempotency key or operation identifier, executing the mutation only if that key is absent from the state ledger or journal. Why this gets asked: evaluates practical mechanism design rather than memorized terminology. Why is a “check then act” pattern not atomic? Between the check and the mutation, a concurrent process can modify the state (TOCTOU race condition); you must use atomic primitives or explicit locking. Why this gets asked: classic concurrency race condition question at the systems level. How do you bound the total execution time of an entire script rather than individual commands? Rely on external orchestrator or CI job timeouts, complemented by per-operation timeouts; a single timeout command on child processes is insufficient. Why this gets asked: reflects the principle that a hanging task is far worse than a failing task. Why is an unbounded polling loop a defect rather than defensive programming? It converts an operational failure into an indefinite hang: resources remain locked until timeout, and alerting signals arrive delayed and obscured. Why this gets asked: probes your attitude toward failure modes and timeout handling. How do you design a script that can be safely terminated midway through execution? Decouple preparation from activation: stage all changes in temporary directories, then swap or activate state in a single atomic step (e.g., ln -sfn or mv -T). Why this gets asked: foundation of zero-downtime atomic deployments. What strategy do you use if a script must execute across heterogeneous Linux distributions? Define an explicit baseline of prerequisite capabilities and validate them upfront, rather than blindly assuming utility availability or flag parity. Why this gets asked: portability as an intentional design decision rather than accidental luck. How do you test shell code? Statically via shellcheck; functionally using bats or containerized ephemeral test runs against synthetic data; and mandatorily via --dry-run modes. Why this gets asked: verifies whether testing is an ingrained engineering discipline for shell code. Should a twenty-line script be covered by automated tests? Usually not: static analysis and a dry-run flag suffice. Automated test suites are justified when logic contains branching conditionals and high failure cost. Why this gets asked: evaluates engineering proportionality against dogmatic testing. How do you recognize when a monolithic script should be decomposed? When distinct subcomponents change for different business reasons or are invoked by different consumers; line count alone is a secondary indicator. Why this gets asked: classic single responsibility principle applied to systems scripting."],["15.html#reliability","What is idempotence in the context of shell scripting?","Questions · IGL","What is idempotence in the context of shell scripting? Executing the script repeatedly yields the same system state without introducing errors, since retries are inevitable in distributed systems. Why this gets asked: central property of robust infrastructure automation."],["15.html#reliability","How do you make a non-idempotent operation safe for retries?","Questions · IGL","How do you make a non-idempotent operation safe for retries? Introduce an idempotency key or operation identifier, executing the mutation only if that key is absent from the state ledger or journal. Why this gets asked: evaluates practical mechanism design rather than memorized terminology."],["15.html#reliability","Why is a “check then act” pattern not atomic?","Questions · IGL","Why is a “check then act” pattern not atomic? Between the check and the mutation, a concurrent process can modify the state (TOCTOU race condition); you must use atomic primitives or explicit locking. Why this gets asked: classic concurrency race condition question at the systems level."],["15.html#reliability","How do you bound the total execution time of an entire script rather than individual commands?","Questions · IGL","How do you bound the total execution time of an entire script rather than individual commands? Rely on external orchestrator or CI job timeouts, complemented by per-operation timeouts; a single timeout command on child processes is insufficient. Why this gets asked: reflects the principle that a hanging task is far worse than a failing task."],["15.html#reliability","Why is an unbounded polling loop a defect rather than defensive programming?","Questions · IGL","Why is an unbounded polling loop a defect rather than defensive programming? It converts an operational failure into an indefinite hang: resources remain locked until timeout, and alerting signals arrive delayed and obscured. Why this gets asked: probes your attitude toward failure modes and timeout handling."],["15.html#reliability","How do you design a script that can be safely terminated midway through execution?","Questions · IGL","How do you design a script that can be safely terminated midway through execution? Decouple preparation from activation: stage all changes in temporary directories, then swap or activate state in a single atomic step (e.g., ln -sfn or mv -T). Why this gets asked: foundation of zero-downtime atomic deployments."],["15.html#reliability","What strategy do you use if a script must execute across heterogeneous Linux distributions?","Questions · IGL","What strategy do you use if a script must execute across heterogeneous Linux distributions? Define an explicit baseline of prerequisite capabilities and validate them upfront, rather than blindly assuming utility availability or flag parity. Why this gets asked: portability as an intentional design decision rather than accidental luck."],["15.html#reliability","How do you test shell code?","Questions · IGL","How do you test shell code? Statically via shellcheck; functionally using bats or containerized ephemeral test runs against synthetic data; and mandatorily via --dry-run modes. Why this gets asked: verifies whether testing is an ingrained engineering discipline for shell code."],["15.html#reliability","Should a twenty-line script be covered by automated tests?","Questions · IGL","Should a twenty-line script be covered by automated tests? Usually not: static analysis and a dry-run flag suffice. Automated test suites are justified when logic contains branching conditionals and high failure cost. Why this gets asked: evaluates engineering proportionality against dogmatic testing."],["15.html#reliability","How do you recognize when a monolithic script should be decomposed?","Questions · IGL","How do you recognize when a monolithic script should be decomposed? When distinct subcomponents change for different business reasons or are invoked by different consumers; line count alone is a secondary indicator. Why this gets asked: classic single responsibility principle applied to systems scripting."],["15.html#security-blast","Security and blast radius (31–40)","Questions · IGL","Security and blast radius (31–40) What is a script’s blast radius and how do you constrain it? The maximum damage possible during failure; constrained by scoping allowed target paths via strict allowlists and limiting the volume of mutations per run. Why this gets asked: tests worst-case architectural risk modeling. Why are synchronized retries across hundreds of nodes dangerous during external service degradation? Simultaneous retries hammer the recovering service, triggering a thundering herd failure. Implement retries with exponential backoff and jitter, and apply randomized start delays to cron jobs ( RandomizedDelaySec in systemd timers). Why this gets asked: evaluates single-node blast radius multiplied across an entire fleet. What must be strictly enforced during shell code reviews versus left to personal preference? Enforce what machines catch ( shellcheck with no global suppressions), exit codes, scope constraints on destructive operations, and retry semantics. Leave formatting to shfmt. Debating formatting during review wastes team bandwidth. Why this gets asked: evaluates your ability to build engineering review processes. Why is curl … | bash considered an antipattern? Code executes uninspected, and executes incrementally: the shell interprets lines as chunks arrive over the socket, so a network disruption can leave half the script executed and truncate a command into an unintended operation. Without pipefail, download failures are completely ignored. Why this gets asked: combines supply chain trust analysis with deep pipeline stream mechanics. Which operations should never be delegated to autonomous AI agents without human confirmation? Deletions, privilege escalation, downloading and executing network binaries, and rewriting shared VCS history - actions that are irreversible or disrupt others. Why this gets asked: timely topic regarding safe autonomous operational boundaries. How do you minimize the privilege envelope of a running script? Run under dedicated unprivileged service accounts, grant fine-grained sudo command permissions rather than root shells, and restrict accessible filesystem trees. Why this gets asked: practical application of the principle of least privilege. How do you protect a script downloading binary assets from tampering? Pin exact release versions and verify cryptographic checksums recorded in the source repository prior to execution. Relying on “latest release” is trusting an unverified external third party. Why this gets asked: essential software supply chain security hygiene. What must you do if a secret is accidentally committed to a repository or printed to CI logs? Immediately treat the secret as compromised and revoke/rotate it; scrubbing git history or deleting log lines does not undo potential exposure. Why this gets asked: evaluates standard security incident response discipline. How do you ensure auditability for script actions? Emit structured logs to stderr including execution run IDs, and stream them to centralized log aggregation infrastructure. Why this gets asked: evaluates operational traceability and compliance. Why does an allowlist of command names fail to secure an agent or user environment? Innocuous-looking utilities can execute arbitrary code or destroy data: find -delete, tar --checkpoint-action, git -c core.pager=…, env, or any language runtime. Real security boundaries require sandbox isolation and inspecting full argument vectors. Why this gets asked: examines where true system security boundaries exist."],["15.html#security-blast","What is a script’s blast radius and how do you constrain it?","Questions · IGL","What is a script’s blast radius and how do you constrain it? The maximum damage possible during failure; constrained by scoping allowed target paths via strict allowlists and limiting the volume of mutations per run. Why this gets asked: tests worst-case architectural risk modeling."],["15.html#security-blast","Why are synchronized retries across hundreds of nodes dangerous during external service degradation?","Questions · IGL","Why are synchronized retries across hundreds of nodes dangerous during external service degradation? Simultaneous retries hammer the recovering service, triggering a thundering herd failure. Implement retries with exponential backoff and jitter, and apply randomized start delays to cron jobs ( RandomizedDelaySec in systemd timers). Why this gets asked: evaluates single-node blast radius multiplied across an entire fleet."],["15.html#security-blast","What must be strictly enforced during shell code reviews versus left to personal preference?","Questions · IGL","What must be strictly enforced during shell code reviews versus left to personal preference? Enforce what machines catch ( shellcheck with no global suppressions), exit codes, scope constraints on destructive operations, and retry semantics. Leave formatting to shfmt. Debating formatting during review wastes team bandwidth. Why this gets asked: evaluates your ability to build engineering review processes."],["15.html#security-blast","Why is curl … | bash considered an antipattern?","Questions · IGL","Why is curl … | bash considered an antipattern? Code executes uninspected, and executes incrementally: the shell interprets lines as chunks arrive over the socket, so a network disruption can leave half the script executed and truncate a command into an unintended operation. Without pipefail, download failures are completely ignored. Why this gets asked: combines supply chain trust analysis with deep pipeline stream mechanics."],["15.html#security-blast","Which operations should never be delegated to autonomous AI agents without human confirmation?","Questions · IGL","Which operations should never be delegated to autonomous AI agents without human confirmation? Deletions, privilege escalation, downloading and executing network binaries, and rewriting shared VCS history - actions that are irreversible or disrupt others. Why this gets asked: timely topic regarding safe autonomous operational boundaries."],["15.html#security-blast","How do you minimize the privilege envelope of a running script?","Questions · IGL","How do you minimize the privilege envelope of a running script? Run under dedicated unprivileged service accounts, grant fine-grained sudo command permissions rather than root shells, and restrict accessible filesystem trees. Why this gets asked: practical application of the principle of least privilege."],["15.html#security-blast","How do you protect a script downloading binary assets from tampering?","Questions · IGL","How do you protect a script downloading binary assets from tampering? Pin exact release versions and verify cryptographic checksums recorded in the source repository prior to execution. Relying on “latest release” is trusting an unverified external third party. Why this gets asked: essential software supply chain security hygiene."],["15.html#security-blast","What must you do if a secret is accidentally committed to a repository or printed to CI logs?","Questions · IGL","What must you do if a secret is accidentally committed to a repository or printed to CI logs? Immediately treat the secret as compromised and revoke/rotate it; scrubbing git history or deleting log lines does not undo potential exposure. Why this gets asked: evaluates standard security incident response discipline."],["15.html#security-blast","How do you ensure auditability for script actions?","Questions · IGL","How do you ensure auditability for script actions? Emit structured logs to stderr including execution run IDs, and stream them to centralized log aggregation infrastructure. Why this gets asked: evaluates operational traceability and compliance."],["15.html#security-blast","Why does an allowlist of command names fail to secure an agent or user environment?","Questions · IGL","Why does an allowlist of command names fail to secure an agent or user environment? Innocuous-looking utilities can execute arbitrary code or destroy data: find -delete, tar --checkpoint-action, git -c core.pager=…, env, or any language runtime. Real security boundaries require sandbox isolation and inspecting full argument vectors. Why this gets asked: examines where true system security boundaries exist."],["15.html#lifecycle","Operations and lifecycle (41–50)","Questions · IGL","Operations and lifecycle (41–50) What must a script report about itself in production? Outcome, exit code, elapsed duration, and volume of processed work - the baseline metrics needed to graph trends and detect gradual degradation. Why this gets asked: tests whether you design with observability in mind. Why is key-value formatted logging preferred over unformatted prose? It is parsed effortlessly by log collectors without complex regular expressions and does not break when human-facing wording is modified. Why this gets asked: practical systems logging discipline. How do you distinguish system degradation from outright failure in script telemetry? Through duration and volume: outright failure is signaled by exit code, while degradation is only visible through trending increases in runtime or drops in throughput. Why this gets asked: evaluates understanding of the difference between monitoring metrics and discrete alerts. How do you decommission a legacy script in production? Through a proxy phase: log deprecation notices with explicit sunset dates to stderr and forward calls to the new tool until upstream callers disappear from logs. Why this gets asked: the most frequently ignored phase of software lifecycle management. How do you safely replace a mission-critical 1000-line legacy script with a dedicated service or tool without downtime? Incrementally: extract isolated high-complexity sections into standalone utilities called from the legacy script, run the new service in parallel shadow mode, compare outputs, and cut over only after zero discrepancies are verified. Why this gets asked: checks experience with safe migration patterns avoiding big-bang cutovers. How do you identify all upstream consumers invoking your script? Log incoming invocations along with caller environment and process metadata to central logging; repository code searches frequently miss ad-hoc crons and dynamic callers. Why this gets asked: pragmatic operational telemetry over theoretical guesswork. How do you safely approach a massive legacy script that everyone is terrified to touch? Surround it with automated safety nets first: enable shellcheck, add a dry-run mode, establish integration regression tests, and only then begin refactoring. Why this gets asked: classic interview question on managing legacy technical debt. How do you transfer ownership of an automation script to another team? Through --help output, header documentation, and an Architecture Decision Record (ADR) explaining why it exists and when it should be retired. Why this gets asked: assesses whether documentation is treated as an integral deliverable. How do you prevent a “one-off temporary script” from surviving for five years? Assign an explicit owner, set an expiration date in the header, place it in an audited location, and on its first reuse make an explicit decision: retire it or promote it to a maintained tool. Temporary scripts become critical legacy not by design, but because nobody made the decision not to. Why this gets asked: evaluates handling of temporary operational work that often becomes technical debt. What indicator tells you that automation should not be built at all? The task occurs rarely, takes only minutes, and demands human contextual judgment at every step: automating it costs far more than doing the manual work. Why this gets asked: tests whether you know when NOT to build software."],["15.html#lifecycle","What must a script report about itself in production?","Questions · IGL","What must a script report about itself in production? Outcome, exit code, elapsed duration, and volume of processed work - the baseline metrics needed to graph trends and detect gradual degradation. Why this gets asked: tests whether you design with observability in mind."],["15.html#lifecycle","Why is key-value formatted logging preferred over unformatted prose?","Questions · IGL","Why is key-value formatted logging preferred over unformatted prose? It is parsed effortlessly by log collectors without complex regular expressions and does not break when human-facing wording is modified. Why this gets asked: practical systems logging discipline."],["15.html#lifecycle","How do you distinguish system degradation from outright failure in script telemetry?","Questions · IGL","How do you distinguish system degradation from outright failure in script telemetry? Through duration and volume: outright failure is signaled by exit code, while degradation is only visible through trending increases in runtime or drops in throughput. Why this gets asked: evaluates understanding of the difference between monitoring metrics and discrete alerts."],["15.html#lifecycle","How do you decommission a legacy script in production?","Questions · IGL","How do you decommission a legacy script in production? Through a proxy phase: log deprecation notices with explicit sunset dates to stderr and forward calls to the new tool until upstream callers disappear from logs. Why this gets asked: the most frequently ignored phase of software lifecycle management."],["15.html#lifecycle","How do you safely replace a mission-critical 1000-line legacy script with a dedicated service or tool without downtime?","Questions · IGL","How do you safely replace a mission-critical 1000-line legacy script with a dedicated service or tool without downtime? Incrementally: extract isolated high-complexity sections into standalone utilities called from the legacy script, run the new service in parallel shadow mode, compare outputs, and cut over only after zero discrepancies are verified. Why this gets asked: checks experience with safe migration patterns avoiding big-bang cutovers."],["15.html#lifecycle","How do you identify all upstream consumers invoking your script?","Questions · IGL","How do you identify all upstream consumers invoking your script? Log incoming invocations along with caller environment and process metadata to central logging; repository code searches frequently miss ad-hoc crons and dynamic callers. Why this gets asked: pragmatic operational telemetry over theoretical guesswork."],["15.html#lifecycle","How do you safely approach a massive legacy script that everyone is terrified to touch?","Questions · IGL","How do you safely approach a massive legacy script that everyone is terrified to touch? Surround it with automated safety nets first: enable shellcheck, add a dry-run mode, establish integration regression tests, and only then begin refactoring. Why this gets asked: classic interview question on managing legacy technical debt."],["15.html#lifecycle","How do you transfer ownership of an automation script to another team?","Questions · IGL","How do you transfer ownership of an automation script to another team? Through --help output, header documentation, and an Architecture Decision Record (ADR) explaining why it exists and when it should be retired. Why this gets asked: assesses whether documentation is treated as an integral deliverable."],["15.html#lifecycle","How do you prevent a “one-off temporary script” from surviving for five years?","Questions · IGL","How do you prevent a “one-off temporary script” from surviving for five years? Assign an explicit owner, set an expiration date in the header, place it in an audited location, and on its first reuse make an explicit decision: retire it or promote it to a maintained tool. Temporary scripts become critical legacy not by design, but because nobody made the decision not to. Why this gets asked: evaluates handling of temporary operational work that often becomes technical debt."],["15.html#lifecycle","What indicator tells you that automation should not be built at all?","Questions · IGL","What indicator tells you that automation should not be built at all? The task occurs rarely, takes only minutes, and demands human contextual judgment at every step: automating it costs far more than doing the manual work. Why this gets asked: tests whether you know when NOT to build software."],["15.html#artifacts","Artifacts: what you take away from this page","Questions · IGL","Artifacts: what you take away from this page Reading >> Your life quantum · Dignity partnership with AI · Rewiring your neural network Fifty architectural decisions with explicit trade-offs. Your time was well spent if you began answering technical questions with preconditions: “if the task manages state, then...”, “if there is more than one caller, then...”. That is the essence of rewiring your neural network: an answer stops being an arbitrary technology choice and becomes an accountable architectural decision. Applicability criteria: action versus state, and total cost of ownership. Tool contracts and principles of unbroken backward compatibility. Idempotence, race conditions, and interruptions as intentional design targets. Blast radius, allowlists, and least privilege - designing from the worst case. Observability and decommissioning with explicit sunset dates and proxying phases. Final bank page. The full site map is on the landing page."],["glossary.html#block-mechanics","Mechanics: how the shell executes a command line","Glossary","Mechanics: how the shell executes a command line"],["glossary.html#block-practice","Practice: scripts that survive production","Glossary","Practice: scripts that survive production"],["glossary.html#block-decisions","Decisions: bounding the worst case","Glossary","Decisions: bounding the worst case"],["glossary.html#artifacts","Artifacts: what you take away from this glossary","Glossary","Artifacts: what you take away from this glossary Reading >> Your quantum of life's time · A partnership of dignity with AI · Rewiring your neural network A shared vocabulary instead of twenty-two memorized definitions. Time spent on this page is well invested if you and your team mean the exact same thing when saying \"idempotent\", \"atomic\", or \"lost exit status\". That is the neural rewire: a technical term ceases to be just jargon and becomes a tool for substantive engineering discussion. Fifteen mechanics concepts - from shebang to POSIX, ordered as the shell traverses from the file's first line to process exit. Five practice concepts - strict mode, dry run, idempotence, atomicity, locking. Two architectural decision concepts - tool contract and blast radius, neither of which is found in man bash. The glossary expands alongside the site sections. The complete description of all mechanisms is in man bash."],["glossary.html#shebang","Shebang #!/usr/bin/env bash","Glossary","The first line of a script, instructing the kernel which interpreter to execute it with. Without it, the script runs in whichever shell invoked it - and on a machine where sh is dash, arrays and [[ ]] stop working. The env form searches for bash in PATH rather than only in /bin: on macOS and the BSDs it resides in a different location. First seen in: Writing scripts · Skeleton"],["glossary.html#shell-modes","Shell modes login · interactive","Glossary","A single Bash program in two roles: an interactive shell used by a human, and an interpreter executing a file. An interactive shell reads ~/.bashrc, enables aliases, and maintains history; a login shell (SSH sessions, su -) reads ~/.bash_profile; a non-interactive script reads neither. Hence \"it works in my terminal, but fails in cron\": PATH and aliases from your profile files never reach the script. First seen in: Writing scripts · Two modes"],["glossary.html#path","PATH $PATH","Glossary","A colon-separated list of directories where the shell searches for executables by name. The current directory is not included, which is why a local script must be run as./script.sh. Order matters: the first matching executable is executed, and type cmd reveals which one it is. In cron and CI environments, PATH is typically shorter than in your interactive shell - a script relying on implicit locations will fail there first. First seen in: Examples · Noob · First script"],["glossary.html#environment-variable","Environment variable export VAR=value","Glossary","A variable exported by the shell to child processes. A standard assignment VAR=value remains visible only within the current shell process; export marks the variable for inheritance. Inheritance flows strictly downward: a child process cannot modify its parent process's environment. That is why values set in one CI step do not persist into the next - each step runs in a separate process. First seen in: Key components · Variables"],["glossary.html#glob","Glob *.log ·?.txt · [ab]*","Glossary","An expression expanded by the shell into matching filenames before executing the command. Filename expansion is performed by the shell itself rather than the utility, preserving filenames containing whitespace intact - unlike parsing $(ls). If no file matches, by default the pattern remains a literal string, and a loop executes once with *.log; shopt -s nullglob changes this behavior to expand to an empty list instead. First seen in: Key components · Command line parsing"],["glossary.html#word-splitting","Word splitting \"$var\"","Glossary","Dividing unquoted expansion results into distinct words based on whitespace and newlines. Bash substitutes the variable value and then splits it on delimiter characters. Consequently, an unquoted path like /home/My Files/a.txt is split into two separate arguments, causing a delete command to remove unintended files. Enclosing expansions in double quotes is not a style preference but a defensive necessity - and it remains the most common error made by AI coding agents. First seen in: Key components · Expansions and word splitting"],["glossary.html#ifs","IFS IFS=$'\\n\\t'","Glossary","The Internal Field Separator: the set of characters the shell uses to split text into words. By default, it contains space, tab, and newline. Restricting IFS to newline and tab at the start of a script prevents unwanted splitting on spaces where it is rarely intended. In the idiom IFS= read -r line, the assignment applies only to the single command, preserving leading and trailing whitespace. First seen in: Key components · Expansions and word splitting"],["glossary.html#stream-fd","Stream and file descriptor 0 · 1 · 2","Glossary","A numbered input or output channel for a process: 0 is standard input, 1 is standard output, 2 is standard error. A file descriptor is an integer; a stream is the data flowing through it. Separating stdout (1) and stderr (2) is a fundamental prerequisite for pipelines: downstream commands read only stream 1, so informational messages printed there pollute pipeline data. Hence the rule: data goes to stdout, operational logging to stderr, execution status to the exit code. First seen in: Key components · Processes and redirections"],["glossary.html#redirection","Redirection > · >> · 2>&1","Glossary","Instructing the shell to redirect a process stream away from the default terminal. > truncates and overwrites a file, >> appends to it, and 2>&1 redirects standard error to wherever standard output is currently directed. Redirections are evaluated strictly from left to right, which is why cmd >log 2>&1 and cmd 2>&1 >log behave differently: in the latter case, errors continue printing to the terminal. First seen in: Key components · Processes and redirections"],["glossary.html#pipeline","Pipeline a | b","Glossary","A chain of commands where the output of each process feeds directly into the input of the next. Pipeline stages execute concurrently in separate subshell processes rather than sequentially. By default, the overall exit status of the pipeline is that of the final command alone - causing failing_command | tee log to return 0. The complete array of return codes for all pipeline stages is preserved in ${PIPESTATUS[@]}. First seen in: Key components · Pipelines and exit statuses"],["glossary.html#exit-code","Exit status $?","Glossary","A status byte from 0 to 255 signaling command success (0) or failure (non-zero). It is the sole machine-readable indicator of success or failure: stderr messages are meant for human operators, while exit codes are meant for automation. An error message unaccompanied by a non-zero exit code means CI registers a passing step. Codes 126, 127, and 128+N are issued directly by the shell: \"permission denied/not executable\", \"command not found\", and \"terminated by signal N\". First seen in: Key components · Pipelines and exit statuses"],["glossary.html#subshell","Subshell (...)","Glossary","A child shell process that inherits the environment, but whose modifications never propagate back to the parent. Hence the classic pitfall: a loop consuming output from a pipe counts items, but once the loop terminates, the counter variable is empty again - the loop executed within an isolated subshell. Subshells are spawned by parentheses, pipeline stages, and $() command substitutions; curly braces do not create subshells. Understanding this boundary explains lost cd directory changes and exit calls that fail to terminate the enclosing script. First seen in: Key components · Variables and scopes"],["glossary.html#signal","Signal SIGINT · SIGTERM · SIGKILL","Glossary","An asynchronous notification delivered to a process by the kernel, another process, or the controlling terminal. SIGINT is dispatched by Ctrl+C, SIGTERM by kill and container orchestrator stops, and SIGKILL by kill -9. Processes can trap the first two to perform graceful shutdown; SIGKILL cannot be trapped or ignored because it is handled directly by the kernel. A process terminated by signal N returns exit status 128+N: 130 for Ctrl+C, 143 for SIGTERM. First seen in: Key components · Signals and traps"],["glossary.html#trap","Trap trap … EXIT","Glossary","An action registered with the shell to execute upon receiving an OS signal or internal runtime event. A trap on EXIT fires whenever the shell process terminates normally, on error, or via an interrupt, making it the only dependable mechanism for cleaning up temporary resources. The sole exception is SIGKILL: the kernel destroys the process immediately, bypassing user space traps entirely. Traps on ERR execute on command failure; ensuring they are inherited by shell functions and subshells requires set -E. First seen in: Key components · Signals and traps"],["glossary.html#posix","POSIX and bashisms #!/bin/sh","Glossary","POSIX defines the baseline specification common across Unix shells; bashisms are features specific to Bash. Arrays, [[ ]] conditionals, process substitution, and ${v//a/b} parameter expansions are bashisms: they fail under dash, which is symlinked to /bin/sh on Debian and Ubuntu. Choose deliberately from the start: either use #!/usr/bin/env bash with full feature access, or choose #!/bin/sh, validate with shellcheck -s sh, and avoid non-standard syntax. First seen in: Weaknesses and limits · Portability"],["glossary.html#strict-mode","Strict mode set -euo pipefail","Glossary","Three safety fuses: abort on error, abort on unset variable, do not mask failures inside pipelines. -e exits on non-zero return codes, but is ignored inside conditions, &&, || lists, function calls in conditionals, and local x=$(cmd). -u treats uninitialized variables as fatal errors rather than silently substituting an empty string. -o pipefail propagates the exit status of the rightmost failing pipeline command. It establishes a necessary hygiene baseline, not an absolute guarantee of script safety. First seen in: Writing scripts · Skeleton"],["glossary.html#dry-run","Dry run --dry-run","Glossary","An execution mode where the script prints planned mutations without executing them. It provides reliable guarantees only when engineered systematically: every state-mutating command routes through a single wrapper function that prints instead of executing when dry run is active. Retrofitted haphazardly, it inevitably misses dangerous branches. Furthermore, wrappers cannot prevent shell redirection side effects: run cmd > file truncates the target file even in dry-run mode because > is processed by the shell prior to function execution. For AI agents, dry run is the principal method for validating logic safely; for engineers, it enables review before changes take effect. First seen in: Examples · Pro · Options and --dry-run"],["glossary.html#idempotence","Idempotence mkdir -p · ln -sfn","Glossary","A property of an operation: executing it repeatedly yields the same result without corrupting system state. In automated environments, retries are inevitable: network timeouts, node restarts, duplicate events, or an autonomous agent repeating steps. Commands like mkdir -p, ln -sfn, and rsync are idempotent; bare mkdir and unmanaged >> append operations are not. Non-idempotent operations require unique operation identifiers, running only when that identifier is absent from the execution ledger. First seen in: Writing scripts · Idempotence and cleanup"],["glossary.html#atomicity","Atomicity mv -T","Glossary","A property of an operation: it completes entirely or has no effect, exposing no intermediate state to observers. Renaming a file on the same filesystem is atomic; copying is not. For this reason, output files should be generated in a temporary staging path and swapped into place with an atomic mv. Similarly, switching a symlink to a new release is done via ln -sfn to a temporary path followed by mv -T over the active link. The sequence of rm followed by ln -s is not atomic: between the two steps, no link exists. First seen in: DevOps practices · Deployment and rollback"],["glossary.html#locking","Locking flock","Glossary","A concurrency guard ensuring that multiple instances of the same task never execute simultaneously. flock maintains an exclusive lock tied to an open file descriptor, which the operating system automatically releases when the process terminates - including on unexpected crashes. Primitive PID file-based locks lack this kernel protection: after an unhandled crash, stale lock files remain on disk, blocking subsequent runs indefinitely. Its standard application is a scheduled cron job that might overrun its execution window. First seen in: Examples · Pro · Single instance"],["glossary.html#tool-contract","Tool contract stdout · stderr · codes · --json","Glossary","The public runtime contract of a script upon which calling automation and external systems rely. A script contract specifies stream conventions, valid exit codes, structured output formats, dry-run flags, and backward compatibility guarantees. Implementation logic may be refactored freely, but the public contract cannot: external systems depend on it, and unannounced changes break downstream consumers without warning. Custom exit codes should be selected from the 3-63 range or align with sysexits.h, leaving codes 126, 127, and 128+N reserved for shell and signal semantics. First seen in: Examples · IGL · Script contract"],["glossary.html#blast-radius","Blast radius blast radius","Glossary","The maximum potential damage a script can cause when it fails or encounters unexpected input. Robust scripts are engineered around the worst-case scenario rather than optimistic assumptions. Blast radius is constrained by two checks at the very start: an allowlist bounding the operational target (restricting execution to explicit directory paths or resource identifiers) and an upper limit on items modified per invocation. A script that resolves three thousand targets instead of five must abort immediately and alert a human: this transforms a catastrophic outage into an ordinary operational incident. First seen in: Examples · IGL · Blast radius"],["index.html#seven-goals","What this site holds","Bash: what the assessment asks, and what production needs","What this site holds Seven goals form the common frame of the whole AI Factory series: key components, practices in an AI context, interconnections, weaknesses, examples by maturity level, a bank of 150 questions, and extreme brevity. On the Bash track they are laid out across fifteen sections. All fifteen sections are written in Russian; the English translation is in preparation. A section marked “in Russian” opens the Russian page - saying so is more honest than hiding the link. How Bash is built Key components · English The anatomy: command-line parsing, expansion and word splitting Word splitting × Splitting the result of an expansion into words on whitespace - the cause of most breakage on paths with spaces. Open in glossary, processes and redirection, pipelines Pipeline × A chain of commands joined by |: each one's output is the next one's input. All stages start at once. Open in glossary, variables and scope, exit codes Exit code × A number 0–255 by which a command reports success (0) or failure (non-zero). The only machine-readable sign of failure. Open in glossary, signals and traps. Writing scripts · in Russian Rules and techniques: script structure, shebang Shebang × A script's first line, #!/usr/bin/env bash: it tells the kernel which interpreter to run it with. Open in glossary, strict mode, functions and arguments, idempotence, logging, shellcheck. Separately - the two modes of Bash: the interactive shell, where a human types commands, and the script interpreter, where the same language runs with nobody watching. Half of the “unexplainable” differences between a terminal and a script come from that boundary. An honest assessment The strength of Bash · in Russian Why it is still everywhere: an executable of a few megabytes, present by default on any Linux system, instant start-up, direct access to processes and files, and a language still being developed. This section exists so that the comparison with the weaknesses is honest rather than one-sided. Weaknesses and limits · in Russian A comparison with Python, Go and zsh/fish. What is genuinely bad: error handling, arrays, floating-point maths, parallelism, readability past three hundred lines. Interconnections · in Russian Seams with Git hooks, CI, Docker ( ENTRYPOINT, no init), systemd, Ansible, cron, Make and Python: where Bash is the glue, and where its zone ends. Practices: four distinct territories Practices in an AI context · in Russian Working with AI agents: how to specify a script task, how to verify the result, why shellcheck inside the agent's loop is worth more than any prompt, and where the model fails routinely - quoting, word splitting, exit codes. Practices in the cloud · in Russian Provisioning and cloud APIs: the CLI as a program, explicit readiness waits, keyless access, batching and throttling, account/region guardrails, and tag-based cleanup. DevOps practices · in Russian Delivery pipelines: Bash on the developer's machine, in build and deploy, in CI/CD and in promotion across Dev → QA → Prod. SRE practices · in Russian Incident triage and production survival: first-minute checklist, why df disagrees with du, zombies and OOM, log analysis, protecting the node via flock and ionice, backoff + jitter, and script metrics. Four boundaries: AI is about agents, cloud is about resources, DevOps is about delivery, SRE is about incidents and load. Maturity levels Examples: Noob · Pro · IGL · in Russian Noob - the foundation without magic. Pro - the real thing: set -euo pipefail, trap, idempotence, logging. IGL - architectural: where a script must give way to a proper tool. Questions: Noob · Pro · IGL · in Russian A bank of 150 questions, fifty per level. Short format: the question, an answer in one or two sentences, and why it gets asked. The seventh goal - extreme brevity - is not a section but a rule of writing that applies to every page: a paragraph that does not change a decision of yours is removed."],["index.html#levels","Three levels, and why they are kept apart","Bash: what the assessment asks, and what production needs","Three levels, and why they are kept apart Noob - you run scripts and occasionally edit them. The level's job: remove the magic. Pro - you write scripts that run on other people's machines. The level's job: remove silent failures. IGL - you decide whether a script should exist at all. The level's job: see the cost of that decision a year ahead. The same question has different correct answers at different levels. That is why the questions are separated rather than merely sorted by difficulty. Why levels are not the same as difficulty Question: “what does set -e do?” Noob: it aborts the script on the first error. Pro: it aborts - but not inside a pipeline Pipeline × A chain of commands joined by |. By default the exit code comes from the last stage only - the first stage's failure is lost. Open in glossary and not in a condition, which is why you need pipefail set -euo pipefail × Three fuses: fail on error, fail on an unset variable, do not lose a failure inside a pipeline. Open in glossary. IGL: it grants a false sense of safety - on long logic that is the signal to move to a language with real exceptions."],["index.html#features","What this site can do","Bash: what the assessment asks, and what production needs","What this site can do Small things that help you read. Your choices are remembered between visits, and the page works without the internet. Site search - the magnifier in the header: sections, questions and glossary, with the matches highlighted. The index is fetched on the first search, so pages open no slower. Term cards - a dashed underline: a definition and a link into the glossary without losing your place. Try the word just below. Four languages, one place - the switch leaves you in the same section. Font size and theme - the three A's and day / night, across the whole page. Copying code - a button in every block. Navigator and quick scrolling - the section you are reading stays highlighted; long pages carry buttons to the top and to the end. Examples verified by running - the code on the Examples pages is executed before publication. Every shebang Shebang × A script's first line, #!/usr/bin/env bash: it tells the kernel which interpreter to run it with. Open in glossary on this site is #!/usr/bin/env bash, and that is not taste: it finds the Bash the user actually has, not only the one in /bin."],["index.html#artifacts","Artifacts: what you have after this page","Bash: what the assessment asks, and what production needs","Artifacts: what you have after this page Reading >> Your quantum of life's time · A partnership of dignity with AI · Rewiring your neural network The time spent on this page is unrecoverable. So it owes you more than it took: not an impression, but a changed decision. “You” means every single person who invested attention here - the engineer before an assessment, the author, the teacher. And “artifacts” are not only files and commits but the reader's rewired neural network: word splitting, once genuinely understood, stays with you long after this site is closed. A map of the subject: the six areas of Bash you will be asked about, and a sense of which one of them is your weak side. A level framework: Noob / Pro / IGL as a scale of your own maturity, not as a label. Six terms that are not explained again anywhere else: shebang, exit code, pipeline, set -euo pipefail, word splitting, subshell. The habit of checking by running: any claim about Bash can be verified in a terminal within seconds - and this site verifies its own examples the same way. Part of the AI Factory series. Built with the same toolchain as the rest: plain HTML and CSS, a bash check, published through GitHub Pages."]]};
